-- allium: 3
-- db.allium
--
-- The processing model of a single database within an XTDB node: transaction submission → log processing → block flushing → query.
-- Compaction job selection and the merge algorithm are compaction.allium's; the compaction message flow is here.
--
-- Scope: one database (e.g. "xtdb" or a secondary).
-- External: the multi-database orchestration layer (attach/detach), and leader election (log-processor-lifecycle.allium).
--
-- Source and replica are always separate logs — an invariant, not a convention.
-- One node holds one role per database at a time, leading or following, and one live index shared between them.
-- A leader feeds that index from its own replica-log READ-BACK; a follower feeds it from the replica messages it reads.
-- The compactor writes L1+ tries to the source log, and the leader forwards them to the replica log.
--
-- Leader pipelining — resolve, append, read back, apply:
--   The leader applies its OWN writes only once it has read them BACK off its own replica log at its own TERM — Raft's ReadIndex.
--   That read-back IS the fence.
--   Four stages: RESOLVE a transaction against what the leader already holds and stage it; APPEND it to the replica log, stamped with the term and its position within the term; READ BACK, this node's own replica read delivering each record to whichever role is live; APPLY what the fence admits.
--   Resolution and apply never interleave, so a transaction is never half-applied.
--   Staging, ownership and the durability handles: live-index.allium. The role lifecycle, the claim and the term: log-processor-lifecycle.allium.
--
-- Two heads, two homes — callers choose:
--   RESOLVED head (on the resolver): the highest transaction resolved, and so staged.
--     Drives resolution — the next external-source tx_id, system-time smoothing, read-your-writes.
--   DURABLE head (on the live index): the highest transaction applied, i.e. read back and imported. The QUERY BASIS.
--   Resolved leads; durable lags by everything staged but not yet applied.
--
-- Two source watermarks, and the tell that they differ:
--   The leader's RESOLVE-side watermark is what gets STAMPED onto each replica record.
--   The DURABLE one lives on the watchers and advances only on apply.
--   Stamping from the watchers instead would regress their monotonicity, because a source transaction resolved behind an external-source transaction applies first and pushes the watermark past the stale stamp.
--
-- A follower has no resolver and no read-back: every message it imports is already durable, so it imports straight into the durable live index and advances the watchers as it goes.
--
-- A query MUST NEVER see a transaction the leader has not read back: external snapshots open at the durable head.
--
-- Date64 values retain their raw epoch milliseconds in source and data files.
-- A same-type Date64 cast and a Date64 extremum preserve the selected raw milliseconds.
-- Date subtraction checks that both epoch days and their difference fit the signed 32-bit result type.
-- New Date64 statistics use the UTC day containing the value.
-- Arrow sorting compares Date64 values by the same UTC day as scalar sorting.
-- A native scan widens a potentially legacy pre-epoch lower bound by one day when it reads Date64 statistics.
-- A native scan with a bounded `_id = ANY` array parameter can use the IIDs of the nonnull elements when each element maps to an entity ID.
-- The SQL equality predicate still evaluates against the resolved rows.
-- The native scan can seek past the remainder of an entity's history after an erase or a visible assertion that covers the requested valid interval from the lower bound of the system interval.
-- A redacted event participates at its original system position and valid interval.
-- Temporal resolution can report the selected rectangles of a redacted event or a retraction along with the selected rectangles of values.
-- Value scans refuse when a selected fragment belongs to a redacted event.
-- Value-predicate statistics preserve pages that may contribute a redacted winner.
-- Redaction by itself confers no entity-wide shortcut.
-- In an owned IID bucket this shortcut requires the exact requested entity key.
-- A scan narrowed to one ordinary IID can also skip loading pages whose greatest system time is older than a covering assertion.
-- Ordered indexes use a new page namespace when their Date64 key encoding switches to UTC day flooring.
-- Native writes derive a keyword IID from the bare name.
--
--
-- One leader kind, not two.
--   An external source is an OPTIONAL COLLABORATOR of the leader rather than a separate processor: flush, tries and block coordination and the whole resolve → append → read back → apply pipeline are identical either way.
--   The two differ only in where a transaction enters and how its tx_id is assigned.
--     - No external source: client-driven. The leader subscribes to the source log, resolves transaction messages, and takes each tx_id from the source-log position.
--     - External source configured: transactions arrive through the indexing callback, and tx_id is assigned off the RESOLVED head.
--       A client submit is rejected with xtdb/submit-tx-to-external-source-db.
--       The source log is still consumed, for flush, tries and block coordination only.

use "./trie-cat.allium" as trie_cat
use "./compaction.allium" as compaction

---- Given

given {
    database: Database
    log_processor: LogProcessor
    tx_resolver: TxResolver?           -- leader-only; absent while following
    live_index: LiveIndex              -- shared across both roles
    watchers: Watchers                 -- correlates source msg_ids and tx_ids with waiting clients
    table_catalog: TableCatalog
    compactor: Compactor               -- a peer of the log processor, not a part of it
    storage_format: StorageFormat      -- this node's current storage format
}

---- External Entities

external entity Client {
    -- The party submitting transactions.
}

external entity Throwable {
    -- An error. Opaque here: the abort cause on a resolved transaction or a caller-fault database operation, surfaced to the client but never inspected by this spec's rules.
}

external entity Log {
    -- An ordered, append-only message stream.
    -- Two separate instances per database: source_log and replica_log, which MUST be distinct logs rather than aliases for one.
}

external entity ObjectStore {
    -- Durable key-value storage for blocks, tries and per-table block files.
}

external entity Compactor {
    -- Compacts a database's tries as a peer of the log processor.
    -- Block adoption signals the compactor when automatic compaction is enabled.
    -- See: compaction.allium.
}

external entity ExternalSource {
    -- A system outside XTDB that feeds a database by change data capture, e.g. Postgres logical replication.
    -- On assignment it is handed an indexing callback and an opaque resume token to start after, and feeds transactions in directly.
    -- The native PostgreSQL direct mirror stages snapshots and logical changes from Arrow arrays.
    -- An external-source database assigns its own monotonically increasing tx_id off the RESOLVED head rather than taking it from the source-log position.
    -- A client submit is rejected for such a database: transactions enter only through the source.
}

---- Entities

entity LiveIndex {
    -- See: live-index.allium. Shared between this node's two roles.
    --
    -- latest_completed_tx — DURABLE head / QUERY BASIS: the highest transaction in the durable live tables, and what external snapshots open at.
    --   On a leader it is advanced by apply on READ-BACK, so it lags the resolved head by everything staged but not yet read back.
    --   On a follower it is advanced by the import itself, with no lag, every message a follower imports being already durable.
    -- The RESOLVED head is NOT a field here: on a leader it lives on the resolver, and the live index stays resolver-agnostic.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?
    is_full: Boolean

    -- Rows APPLIED into the currently-open block, and the gauge the leader cuts on.
    -- A leader inheriting a partially-filled block reads the rows already in it, and so cuts it where the previous one would have.
    block_row_count: Integer

    -- Derived
    has_data: latest_completed_tx != null
}

entity Database {
    source_log: Log
    replica_log: Log

    -- The configured external source, if any.
    -- This — not this node's role — is what rejects a client submit: the source log is shared, so any node's submit path has to reach the same decision whether or not it happens to be the leader.
    -- Opened and closed with the database on each node that could lead it, so one source spans every leader term that node serves.
    -- A node that indexes nothing, or holds the database read-only, can never lead and opens no source.
    external_source: ExternalSource?

    invariant DistinctLogs {
        source_log != replica_log
    }
}

entity LogProcessor {
    -- This node's participation in one database. Starts following, and is always in exactly one role.
    -- log-processor-lifecycle.allium owns the role state machine and the transitions; `mode` here is the coarser view this spec's rules need.
    --
    -- latest_replica_msg_id: the position this node's single replica read has reached — the last record it finished with, applied or discarded.
    --   That read is its sole writer and advances it once per record, so the position spans both roles rather than being tracked by each.
    --   A role that ends mid-apply therefore leaves it where it was, and that record is offered again to whatever replaces the role.
    --   Seeded at open from the last persisted block's boundary position, and lives for as long as the database is open on this node.
    --   The read is started once and nothing restarts it, so once it stops this node consumes no further replica records — and claims none, a claim being made only on an empty read of its own.
    --
    -- The SOURCE-log watermark is deliberately NOT here.
    --   The durable one lives on the watchers, advanced on apply by whichever role is live; the leader additionally keeps a resolve-side one, ahead of it, on the Leader variant.
    --   Two watermarks that lead and lag each other are two fields, not one.
    --
    -- max_leader_term: the READ-SIDE TERM FENCE — the highest leader term seen so far.
    --   A record BELOW it was written by a superseded leader and is discarded (see Fencing).
    --   Folded and tested by this node's replica read, before either role acts on the record: records are read one at a time and awaited, so the fence has a single caller at any moment.
    --   Seeded from the last persisted block's term so that it survives restart.
    --   Scoped to the database on this node for its whole life rather than to a role — log-processor-lifecycle.allium's LeaderTerm carries why.
    --   Beside it, and folded by the same read, is how far through that term this node has got: log-processor-lifecycle.allium's term_position, seeded from the same block's boundary position.
    --
    -- pending_block: the block cut and not yet adopted, holding whatever arrived behind its boundary — absent unless one is in flight.
    --   Whichever role is live holds it: a follower that read the boundary, or a leader that produced the block and is waiting to read its own confirmation back.
    --   A block produced and not adopted MUST be handed to the role that replaces the one holding it, in either direction.
    --   The adopt is what moves this node's catalog past the block, so the confirmation does not read as stale; dropped, it reaches a role with no block to match and stops this node's replica read.
    mode: Leader | Follower
    latest_replica_msg_id: MessageId
    max_leader_term: Integer
    pending_block: PendingBlock?
}

variant Leader : LogProcessor {
    -- The sole writer of this database's replica log for its term.
    -- Resolves source-log messages and external-source transactions, appends them stamped with its term, reads them back and applies them.
    -- Also owns the block cycle, and the trie and block garbage collectors.
    --
    -- A promotion's inherited block is produced through this role, before it reads its first source message, and adopted once it reads an upload of that block back.
    --   The boundary the outgoing leader cut is uploaded under the INCOMING term — see The block cycle.
    --   The records the follower was holding drain at the adopt, each having met the fence at its own position in the log on the way in.
    --
    -- external_source is present iff the database has one configured; it makes this leader externally fed rather than client-driven.
    external_source: ExternalSource?

    -- This leader's fencing term: one above the highest term this node had read when it claimed (see log-processor-lifecycle.allium).
    -- Stamped onto every replica record this term writes, and checked on read-back.
    leader_term: Integer

    -- The position within this term of the last record the term appended: 0, the claim that opened it, at promotion.
    -- Each append takes the next, so the term's records carry consecutive positions from 1 in the one order the term appends them.
    last_term_seq: Integer

    -- RESOLVE-side source watermark: the source-log position as of the last-resolved transaction, and what is stamped onto each replica record.
    -- Leads the durable watermark on the watchers, which advances only on apply, and for a block's covered position only on the adopt.
    last_resolved_src_msg_id: MessageId

    -- The last non-null external-source token seen on the resolve side.
    -- Carried onto a block boundary so that the persisted block holds a live resume point.
    last_resolved_ext_token: ExternalSourceToken?

    -- A block cut is in progress: the boundary has been appended and its upload has not been read back.
    -- Resolution is PAUSED while true, so no transaction interleaves between the boundary and the adopt that closes its block, and the buffer of held records stays empty.
    -- True from promotion where the term inherits a block to produce, so such a term resolves nothing until it has adopted that block.
    block_in_progress: Boolean

    -- Whether this term reads the source log yet.
    -- False from promotion where the term inherits a block, until it adopts that block: the adopt can move the durable watermark past the last applied transaction, and the source is read from the watermark the adopt leaves.
    source_delivery_resumed: Boolean
}

variant Follower : LogProcessor {
    -- Reads the replica log — and is the sole replica-log consumer for this node — feeding the shared live index from messages that are all already durable.
}

entity TxResolver {
    -- The leader's transaction resolver, opaque here: the queue of resolved-but-not-yet-applied transactions, the read-your-writes layering and the per-transaction durability handles are all live-index.allium's.
    -- db.allium does not `use` live-index.allium, so it names only what its own rules need.
    --
    -- It exists for the length of one leader term, seeded from the durable head. Absent while following.
    --
    -- RESOLVED head: the highest transaction resolved, and so staged.
    -- Drives resolution — the next external-source tx_id, system-time smoothing, read-your-writes.
    -- Leads the durable head by exactly the transactions staged but not yet applied.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?

    -- The queue itself is not modelled here.
    -- What db.allium relies on is the ORDER PROPERTY that makes the queue head predictable: resolution order = append order = read-back order, and terms are unique per leader.
    -- An equal-term resolved transaction must match the staged head before apply can import its rows (see LeaderAppliesOwnTx).
}

entity TableCatalog {
    -- What one database knows about its tables on this node, and the block that knowledge is current to.
    -- The two are one value, replaced wholesale, so a reader sees a block index against that block's table metadata and never against the previous block's.
    --
    -- A block's per-table metadata folds in exactly once, on the adopt.
    -- The fold sums row counts, so folding the same block twice double-counts it, and that count is what the per-table block file carries.
    -- The produce computes the same fold locally to build those files and installs none of it.
    latest_block: Block?
    table_metadata: Map<trie_cat/TableRef, TableMetadata>

    -- Derived
    current_block_index: latest_block?.block_index
    boundary_replica_msg_id: latest_block?.boundary_replica_msg_id
    -- Seeds the fence's high-water, which is what makes it survive restart.
    boundary_term_id: latest_block?.term_id ?? 0
    -- Seeds the term position: the boundary's term expects its next record one above this, and where it is absent the next record's position is taken as given.
    boundary_term_seq: latest_block?.boundary_term_seq
}

entity Watchers {
    -- The DURABLE watermarks for one database, plus the external-source resume token where it has one.
    -- Whichever role is live advances them only once a message has been applied.
    -- Clients await a tx_id to learn a transaction's result; a promotion reads a source position from here to resume from.
    --
    -- A block's covered source position is reported on the ADOPT, by whichever role adopts the block: a node that has not recorded the block claims none of the position it covers, so a caller that flushes a block and then awaits this watermark is told of it once the flush is done.
    --
    -- A Kotlin wait that observes a failed state receives an IngestionStoppedException carrying the fault, including when its watermark was reached before the fault.
    -- The native monitor checks its applied watermarks first and reports the fault to requests the applied prefix has yet to satisfy.
    -- Cancelling a wait releases that subscription and leaves the other waits and applied watermarks in place.
    latest_tx_id: MessageId
    latest_source_msg_id: MessageId
    latest_tx_result: TransactionResult?
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?

    -- Monotonicity obligations, enforced as records are applied rather than expressible as state properties:
    --   a transaction notification must supply tx_id strictly greater than latest_tx_id;
    --   every notification must supply a source msg_id at or above latest_source_msg_id.
    --   At or above rather than strictly above: the upload closing a block can carry the same source position as the preceding transaction, where the block was triggered by the row gauge rather than by a flush.
}

external entity StorageFormat {
    -- This node's current storage format, as a version and an epoch.
    -- Every tries-added and block-uploaded message carries its own; a mismatch signals a storage upgrade in progress, and the message's tries are forwarded but not registered locally until this node upgrades.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
}

---- Values

value MessageId {
    -- An epoch and an offset, ordered lexicographically.
    -- Source and replica logs have configured epochs from 0 through 32,767.
    -- An offset of -1 denotes the position before the first record in a log epoch.
    epoch: Integer
    offset: Integer
}

value ExternalSourceToken {
    -- An opaque resume token supplied by an external source, e.g. a Postgres LSN.
    -- Carried through a resolved transaction, a block boundary and a block upload on the replica log, tracked on the watchers, and persisted in the block, so that on restart the source can resume from the last durably persisted position.
}

value TransactionKey {
    -- tx_id is the increasing commit-order position, assigned by the source log or off the RESOLVED head for an external-source database.
    -- system_time is the strictly increasing projection used by temporal SQL.
    -- An ordinary explicit system_time that does not advance the resolved history or leads the append clock aborts the transaction.
    -- An owned explicit time instead witnesses the append observation and uses the ordinary automatic projection.
    -- That projection advances by a chronon when an observed clock ties or regresses.
    -- Native source outcomes retain the unmodified append observation in xt.txs.source_log_time.
    -- Strictness is what lets a read basis name a transaction by its system_time — see snapshot_token.
    tx_id: Integer
    system_time: Timestamp
}

value TransactionResult {
    -- The transaction result row declares a nullable Transit error column for committed and aborted results.
    -- The outcome of a resolved transaction, carried through the resolver's queue head and reported to the watchers on apply.
    tx_key: TransactionKey
    committed: Boolean
    error: Throwable?  -- present iff not committed
}

value Block {
    -- A persisted block in the object store.
    block_index: Integer
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId           -- source log
    boundary_replica_msg_id: MessageId?          -- replica log: the position of this block's boundary
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?  -- the last token seen at block time
    -- The term that wrote this block's boundary.
    -- Persisting it is what makes the read-side fence survive a restart: the fence's high-water is seeded from here.
    term_id: Integer
    -- The boundary's position within that term, which seeds the term position a restarted reader resumes at.
    -- Absent on a block written before positions existed, or whose boundary carried none.
    boundary_term_seq: Integer?
    -- Every table this block covers, and where each one's files live. See contract TableEntries.
    tables: List<TableEntry>
}

value TableEntry {
    -- A table's identity, and the directory under which its files live.
    oid: Integer
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    slug: String
}

value PendingBlock {
    -- The state between a block boundary and the upload that closes it.
    -- Held by whichever role is live, which also holds the replica messages that arrive behind it, up to max_buffered_records.
    -- A held record has already met the fence, at its own position in the log: the hold sits below the fence, so what it stores has been admitted.
    -- Overflow is a fault (xtdb.indexer/follower-buffer-overflow) that the leader's block-cut pause keeps unreachable.
    -- Passed on at every role change, in both directions.
    boundary_msg_id: MessageId
    boundary_message: BlockBoundaryMessage
    buffered_records: List<ReplicaMessage>
    max_buffered_records: Integer
}

value Snapshot {
    as_of: TransactionKey
}

value TableMetadata {
    row_count: Integer?
}

value DbOp {
    -- A database-level side-effect carried within a resolved transaction.
    kind: Attach | Detach
}

variant Attach : DbOp {
    db_name: String
    config: DatabaseConfig
}

variant Detach : DbOp {
    db_name: String
}

---- Source Log Messages

-- Messages on the source log. Written by clients, by the leader (flush-block), and by the block upload and the compactor (tries-added).
-- An append retains its encoded record for as long as the log holds those bytes.
-- The encoder releases temporary memory before handing the record to the log.

entity SourceMessage {
    msg_id: MessageId
    -- An in-memory source log with a configured clock samples it for each transaction in append order.
    -- A failed clock sample rejects the append before assigning a position or publishing a record.
    log_timestamp: Timestamp
    kind: TxMessage | LegacyTxMessage | FlushBlockMessage | TriesAddedMessage
         | ArtefactsAddedMessage | ArtefactsRemovedMessage | AttachDbMessage | DetachDbMessage
}

variant TxMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- Client-submitted transaction operations.
    -- Coalesced SQL argument batches align compatible Arrow columns by position and preserve argument row order in source IPC.
    -- The encoder transfers uniquely owned Arrow IPC payloads into their protobuf fields with their allocation reservations.
    -- Embedded ADBC SQL arguments use canonical source fields and preserve their declared types and row order.
    -- SQL source replay binds Arrow argument rows by position and reads a scalar parameter when evaluation reaches it.
    -- Source encoding writes IID lists from their fixed-size bytes.
    -- Source replay reads IIDs directly from the selected position of a compatible Arrow list.
    -- PATCH replay binds compatible document and IID vectors to the mutation plan as one list of records.
    -- Put and create-only insert replay retain their Arrow document vectors.
    -- Governed Arrow writes fill acceptance-relative binary slots in the selected columns during staging.
    -- A put record without an IID vector derives its IIDs from the document ID cells.
    -- A static INSERT from compatible positional scalar batches projects Arrow arguments into source document vectors in upload order.
    -- A static INSERT with per-row valid-time expressions reads the operands of those expressions and gathers compatible payload columns in Arrow.
    -- Rows are grouped by their evaluated bounds in first-seen order and retain their order within each group.
    -- A document layout retains its schema and column metadata while releasing translation scratch after construction.
    -- Source encoding retains compatible Arrow document vectors and admits reconstructed columns one at a time.
    -- Embedded ADBC bulk ingest and pgwire Arrow COPY submit document columns in Arrow.
    -- An ADBC bind with multiple Arrow pages and one document schema submits them in one transaction in page order.
    -- Compatible clear Root writes replay their Arrow document columns with exact field presence and complete identity.
    -- Acceptance-relative Root writes retain those columns when their validity offsets are placed at the ordered system time.
    -- Root publication and fork admission count the Arrow document rows against the same effect limits as row writes.
    -- A held fork of an exact Root publication orders its new world registration with the Root section and owner controls.
    -- The fork inherits the archived publication's clock while Root witnesses validate at ordering.
    -- A reviewed Proposal landing must consume an offered Root clock slot before any of its effects can publish.
    -- The slot's reading binds the Root writes, ordered predicates and landing outcome.
    -- A fixed period's entity-window witness covers its full half-open validity at the original Root cut.
    -- Nested uploads compact child positions and align child fields to the canonical source layout in Arrow.
    -- These uploads normalize Boolean padding and nullable cells to preserve the source representation.
    -- Compatible Arrow document vectors include nested lists, fixed-size lists and structs.
    -- Admitted bulk uploads can retain date, time and duration vectors through source replay.
    -- Compatible document vectors retain the UUID extension for their UUID values through source encoding and replay.
    -- When an upload adds fields inside a fixed-size list, layout alignment copies only the selected entries and required null padding.
    -- Struct fields added across compatible uploads join in Arrow with typed nulls for earlier documents, including structs inside lists.
    -- Batches with compatible column types retain first-seen column order and represent missing columns as nulls without converting the documents to rows.
    -- Source encoding retains compatible Arrow vectors when the same table's puts also contain row-backed documents.
    -- Differently typed columns can share a dense union in the source message and retain their Arrow representation on replay.
    -- Union children can be compatible nested containers such as lists and structs.
    -- Null and missing values use that union's null leg.
    -- When the allocation budget allows, uploaded union tags for the same value layout share a canonical Arrow child, including signed execution aliases.
    -- A normalized union with one value layout uses that Arrow type and its validity bitmap.
    -- A historical native keyed submission can carry an optional guard envelope with a submission clock sample and guard position.
    -- Current ordinary keyed submissions carry the key in metadata and omit the historical guard envelope.
    -- The key marker is valid from its submission sample, inclusive, until one hour later, exclusive.
    -- For a record carrying the envelope, the failed assertion must have the recorded position to identify a duplicate.
    -- Typed outcome lookup selects the latest marker visible at the retry's system time whose validity extends past the retry's submission sample.
    -- A private replay submission instead carries its engine key, admission sample and exclusive cutoff.
    -- These values are required together when the private replay envelope is present.
    -- Submission refuses a sample at or after the cutoff before appending a source record.
    -- A new permit record identifies its legacy-resolver assertion in the envelope and places that assertion first in its operations.
    -- A supporting resolver checks and consumes the assertion before resolving the user's operations.
    -- A resolver that ignores the new field leaves the assertion in the operation sequence.
    -- An existing marker can resolve the duplicate before operation execution.
    -- Execution of the assertion aborts the transaction before any user operation.
    -- A document union leg retains columns in their first encounter order across document operations for the same table and mutation kind.
    system_time: Timestamp?
    default_tz: ZoneId               -- always present
    user: String?
    user_metadata: Bytes?
}

variant LegacyTxMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- A transaction written by an older node: the whole message is one opaque payload rather than a structured envelope.
    -- Still resolvable, so that a log written by an older node replays.
    payload: Bytes
}

variant FlushBlockMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- The leader sends this to itself to trigger a block flush on timeout, so that the cut goes through resolution in log order like any other boundary trigger.
    -- A manual request reads its expected index from the database's adopted table catalog.
    -- The embedded node can request a flush from each open writable database and await each request's applied source position.
    -- Wire observations preserve the message and the presence of its expected index for replay verification.
    expected_block_idx: Integer?
}

variant TriesAddedMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- New tries available: L0 posted by a block upload, L1+ written by the compactor.
    -- The leader forwards these to the replica log.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
}

variant ArtefactsAddedMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- Artefacts an artefact lane has uploaded, such as search files (fts.allium).
    -- The leader forwards the entries it accepts to the replica log.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    entries: List<ArtefactEntry>
}

variant ArtefactsRemovedMessage : SourceMessage {
    -- Recorded artefacts an artefact lane has retired, such as the search files of a dropped index (fts.allium).
    -- The lane deletes their files once the removal is applied.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    entries: List<ArtefactEntry>
}

value ArtefactEntry {
    trie_key: trie_cat/TrieKey
    record: trie_cat/ArtefactRecord
}

variant AttachDbMessage : SourceMessage {
    db_name: String
    config: DatabaseConfig
}

variant DetachDbMessage : SourceMessage {
    db_name: String
}

---- Replica Log Messages

-- Messages on the replica log, written by the leader for its term.
-- Consumed by every node, the leader itself included on read-back, and by its own follower after a demote.

entity ReplicaMessage {
    msg_id: MessageId
    log_timestamp: Timestamp

    -- The leader term that produced this message; 0 on a record written before terms existed.
    -- Set by the message's CREATOR rather than by the append, and carried on the envelope so that every message kind is fenceable.
    -- This field is the whole fence: there is no fencing token and no write-time failure.
    term_id: Integer

    -- This message's position within its term: 0 on the claim that opens the term, then consecutive from 1 for everything the term's leader writes, in the order it appends them.
    -- Absent on a record written by a node predating positions, which is distinct from 0.
    -- Stamped by the APPEND rather than by the creator, because the position is the order the term's one append path puts its records in.
    term_seq: Integer?

    kind: ResolvedTxMessage | BlockBoundaryMessage | BlockUploadedMessage
         | TriesAddedMessage | TriesDeletedMessage | NoOpMessage
         | ArtefactsAddedMessage | ArtefactsRemovedMessage | OversizedMessage
}

variant ResolvedTxMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- An event written with table history carries its accepted commit identity, within-commit ordinal, and original block.
    -- A resolved transaction: put, delete and erase events that require no database reads.
    -- An attach or detach result is carried as a database operation within it.
    -- For an external-source database it carries the resume token as at the transaction boundary.
    tx_id: MessageId                          -- the source-log position of the original message,
                                              -- or the resolver-assigned tx_id for an external-source database
    system_time: Timestamp                    -- the resolved system time
    committed: Boolean
    error: Throwable?                         -- the abort reason, null if committed
    db_op: DbOp?                              -- an attach or detach side-effect, if any
    table_data: Map<String, Bytes>            -- the resolved row data per table
    -- An admitted public history index can carry a derived token delta in the same accepted record as the table data.
    search_delta: Bytes?
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?

    -- The leader's RESOLVE-side source watermark when this record was produced: for a source-log transaction its own position, for an external-source one the leader's current source-log position, so that followers keep their durable watermark in step between block boundaries.
    -- Null only on records written before the field existed, where readers fall back to the tx_id or to the tracked watermark.
    src_msg_id: MessageId?

    -- A strict placed commit retains its rounded writer staging sample and exclusive staging cutoff inline with its effect and receipt. The sample and resolved system time both precede the cutoff. These coordinates record the admission check before quorum durability.
    staging_clock_upper: Timestamp?
    staging_cutoff: Timestamp?
}

variant BlockBoundaryMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- The leader signals that a block boundary has been reached.
    -- Injected in RESOLUTION order, off the resolve-side row gauge or on a successful flush CAS, so that it lands after this block's transactions and before the next block's.
    -- Whichever role reads it holds a pending block on it and holds the messages behind it until the matching upload; the leader that cut it also produces the block here.
    -- L0 tries are delivered in the subsequent upload rather than separately.
    block_index: Integer
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId           -- the resolve-side source watermark at block time
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?
}

variant BlockUploadedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- The block has been written to the object store.
    -- Carries the L0 tries, so that every node can update its trie catalog.
    -- The role holding the block adopts it on this and moves onto the next block.
    block_index: Integer
    latest_processed_msg_id: MessageId
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
    external_source_token: ExternalSourceToken?
}

variant TriesAddedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- New tries available, forwarded by the leader from the source log.
    -- source_msg_id carries the original source-log position, so that staleness checking and the watchers advance correctly.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    tries: List<trie_cat/TrieDetails>
    source_msg_id: MessageId
}

variant ArtefactsAddedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Artefacts the leader accepted, forwarded from the source log.
    -- Staleness and the watchers treat source_msg_id as they do a tries-added's.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    entries: List<ArtefactEntry>
    source_msg_id: MessageId
}

variant ArtefactsRemovedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Artefact records the leader forwarded from the source log, to be removed from their tries' entries.
    -- Staleness and the watchers treat source_msg_id as they do a tries-added's.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    table: trie_cat/TableRef
    entries: List<ArtefactEntry>
    source_msg_id: MessageId
}

variant TriesDeletedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Tries the leader's trie GC has deleted from the object store, published so that every node drops them from its in-memory catalog at the same logical position.
    -- Written only by the leader, and only once every file in the chunk is gone.
    -- Replay is always safe: the removal is idempotent and never stale.
    -- See: gc.allium.
    table_name: String
    trie_keys: Set<trie_cat/TrieKey>
}

variant NoOpMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Carries no effect of its own, and is used for three things.
    --   1. A leadership claim. A node appends one stamped with a term above the highest it has read, at position 0 in that term; whether that claim confers leadership is settled by where the log put it (see log-processor-lifecycle.allium).
    --   2. A leader's assertion that it is still there, on a database with no traffic to write. A leader with work needs none, its own writes carrying the same term.
    --   3. Carrying the leader's source watermark forward where no other record would — a flush whose CAS fails, and which therefore finishes no block.
    src_msg_id: MessageId?   -- null on a claim and on an assertion
}

variant OversizedMessage : ReplicaMessage {
    -- Stands in for a message the log declined for its size.
    -- The declined message's own encoding is in the object store and this names it, so the indirection is a property of the envelope rather than of any one kind: resolved transactions, tries-added and block-uploaded are all unbounded and all offload through it.
    -- Written by the leader only, and only on a refused append — the log is asked first, and this is what happens when it says no.
    -- term_id and term_seq on the envelope are the DECLINED message's, so read-side fencing decides without fetching the payload.
    -- Readers resolve it as the record is read, ahead of both the pending-block hold and the staleness check: an offloaded upload closes a block, and one left unresolved would be held behind the block it closes.
    -- block_index is the block the standing-in record lands in — the open one, not the last completed — because block-gc.allium collects on it.
    -- New references authenticate the exact encoded body with SHA-256; older references still verify the decoded term and sequence.
    storage_version: Integer
    storage_epoch: Integer
    block_index: Integer
    payload_id: String
}

---- Config

config {
    -- The worst-case interval over which an external source's upstream must retain its log, so the value bounds more than block cadence.
    flush_timeout: Duration = 15.minutes
    rows_per_block: Integer = 102400
    automatic_compaction: Boolean = true
}

---- Rules: Role Transitions

-- == Fencing ==
--
-- There is at most one CONFIRMED leader per database, enforced by a TERM on the read side.
-- There is no write-time fence.
--
-- Every replica record carries the term of the leader that created it.
-- The term is taken from the log itself — a node claims one above the highest it has read — so the terms being compared come from the same durable record every reader is fenced against.
--
-- The fence is applied ONCE to every record, on this node's replica read and before either role acts on it: a record below the highest term seen so far is DISCARDED, and one at or above it folds that high-water upward and, unless its position within the term discards or voids it, is handed on.
-- Records are read one at a time, handed to the live role and awaited, so the fence has a single caller at any moment.
-- The high-water belongs to the database on this node, seeded from the last persisted block, so the fence survives restart.
-- A discarded record still advances the read position, so the read never stalls on one and a claim behind one is still reached.
--
-- The same read folds each record's position within its term; log-processor-lifecycle.allium owns the rule.
-- A term opened by a claim at position 0 is sequenced, and its records have to arrive at consecutive positions from 1.
-- One behind the expected position, or carrying none, is a losing claim or a duplicate, and is DISCARDED.
-- One ahead of it means a record of the term never reached the log, and the rest of the term is VOIDED: consumed, and never applied.
-- A higher term whose first record here is not at position 0 is voided from that record; one opened by a record carrying no position is unsequenced, and nothing in it is checked.
-- A voided record advances the read position as a discarded one does.
-- A LEADER that meets a voided term ends its term where it reads the record, without acknowledging its outstanding writes.
--
-- The fold belongs to the RECORD rather than to the role that acts on it, so it happens on admission, whichever role ends up taking the record.
--
-- An admitted record above the term a LEADER is leading has superseded that term — Raft's ReadIndex: a leader applies and acknowledges a write only once it has read that write back at its own term.
-- That leader is DEMOTED where the record is read, without acknowledging its outstanding writes, and the record is then handed to the follower that replaces it.
-- A follower leads no term, so nothing handed to one supersedes it.
--
-- Every record meets the fence at its own position in the log, the ones held behind an OPEN PENDING BLOCK included: the hold sits BELOW the fence, so a record is admitted before it is held, and the drain applies what the block was holding without consulting the fence again.
-- The block closes on the MATCH — block index, storage version and storage epoch, and never the term, which the fence has settled before the record reaches the match.
-- So an upload written by a term the log has moved past closes nothing: it is discarded like everything else that term wrote, and the block stays open until a term at or above the high-water uploads it again.
--
-- Two nodes may therefore briefly both believe themselves leader and both APPEND.
-- Only the newer term's writes are ever confirmed, and the older leader's are discarded, which is the property that matters — not single-writer-at-the-log.
--
-- The SOURCE log is not fenced.
-- Stale messages from a previous leader (a flush) or from the compactor (tries-added) may appear after a transition, and both are safe:
--   a flush is a CAS on the expected block index, so a stale one fails the check;
--   adding tries is idempotent, so a duplicate is a no-op.

-- == Transition Safety ==
--
-- The end of a term ABANDONS in-flight resolution rather than draining it.
-- A staged-but-not-yet-applied transaction was never visible to an external query, so discarding it costs nothing: the next leader re-reads it from the source log and re-resolves it.
-- What the end of a term MUST do instead is fail everything awaiting it — every staged external-source transaction's durability handle, the paused source batch, and whatever is still queued — because the symptom of missing one is a HANG.
--
-- So the live index still reflects a complete prefix of the replica log: it is only ever advanced by APPLY, one whole transaction at a time, and an abandoned transaction is one that was never applied.

-- == Leader Startup ==
--
-- A writable restart restores the latest persisted block and reads the replica suffix from that block's boundary.
-- An admitted upload after a recovered boundary in the configured replica epoch confirms its checkpoint when the block index, storage version and epoch, source watermark, and external token match its recorded identity.
-- An omitted upload storage epoch means zero.
-- A writable restart also checks contiguous retained older catalogs whose replica history admits the exact selected boundary with a compatible fence.
-- If opening the historical tail reports that its first requested position precedes the retained log, the writer tries the next newer retained catalog when one exists.
-- For a positive-term selected boundary without a sequence, a same-term unsequenced witness satisfies an unknown startup position.
-- Ordinary replica replay uses the fence seeded from the selected checkpoint.
-- The check observes confirmations while keeping the restored partition and applied progress in place.
-- After an elected term observes the retained prefix through its claim, it announces each still-missing confirmation in block order.
-- Each announcement describes the L0 tries recorded for that block in its table blocks.
-- The node waits for each admitted read-back before appending the next announcement.
-- The term confirms the missing uploads before producing a later pending block or resuming ordinary source and external-source processing.
-- Followers still holding a matching boundary adopt and drain on the admitted upload.
-- The restored checkpoint is already installed on the restarted node.
-- A replica that already installed the checkpoint skips its upload before the hold for a later pending block.
-- If producing that later block fails during promotion, the replica follows with the checkpoint confirmed and the later block still pending.
-- The writer checks shared storage for the catalog, table and L0 files before appending an older confirmation.
-- The same fresh storage check applies before submitting a new confirmation of the recovered current checkpoint.
-- A packed file's check requires the containing pack to be present in storage.
-- The pack index confines entries to the payload before the trailer.
-- With provider versions, the pack metadata, trailer, index and each returned member range must name the same physical publication.
-- Hosted publication records the digest of the adopted catalog bytes next to its checkpoint coordinate.
-- A hosted serving receipt also commits to the exact replay inventory, including each logical length and any observed physical version.
-- Its digest covers the root, replica epoch and first required replica offset.
-- The admitted store recomputes the inventory commitment from fresh storage observations before opening the cut.
-- A single fresh observation can cover members that share an admitted pack.
-- A hosted prefix trim requires a checkpoint with this commitment.
-- Startup, cursor recovery and checkpoint suffix admission check the same persisted prefix authorization.
-- An unpublished archived suffix retains its journal interval while its pin is active.
-- Publishing its verified archive transfers that interval to archive custody.
-- A live archive copy admits its first required offset in the same update that opens its provider region.
-- That region retains the original suffix until its provider work drains.
-- Opening a directory receipt verifies fresh root bytes before loading table metadata.
-- An ordinary startup admits the root metadata and first required replica offset in one directory update.
-- The startup grant lasts through cursor admission and the drain of the provider reads it owns.
-- A delivered record in the admitted replay interval retains the grant while a referenced payload is fetched, including after cancellation of its caller.
-- Those files remain required until follower adoption.
-- A failed historical check or older candidate preparation discards the remaining older confirmations.
-- The writer continues recovery of the selected checkpoint.
-- A follower that reopens loads the latest reported checkpoint and applies its retained suffix.
-- In the Rust partition owner, trie collection preserves the data and metadata of live and nascent tries in the latest installed checkpoint.
-- Recovery derives that protection from the restored catalog. Block adoption derives it from the persisted table blocks.
-- Ordinary latest-block recovery refreshes discovery after a missing root or table block.
-- It retries only when the refreshed listing reveals a strictly newer block.
-- With a provider that admits a cursor during recovery, ordinary startup opens the replica tail before installing the checkpoint.
-- A trimmed first required offset triggers refresh and may select only a strictly newer reported checkpoint.
-- An admitted hosted checkpoint binds its block number and replica boundary within one journal.
-- A stored catalog at the admitted block must match the boundary exactly.
-- A successor must advance both the block and the replica boundary in that journal, including on a retry after prefix collection.
-- The opened replica log must belong to the same journal as the admitted checkpoint.
-- On a hosted loglet, each connection verifies the durable identity named by its ensemble slot before sending replay or recovery operations.
-- A new Rust checkpoint root carries the SHA-256 digest of each table block, keyed by the table registry OID.
-- The root is written after every referenced table block write completes.
-- Recovery checks the manifest covers exactly those OIDs and checks the stored bytes before accepting table metadata.
-- Roots without this manifest use the existing table-block load.
-- New archive captures and independent fork copies derive their dependency inventory from the verified table blocks.
-- The replay inventory includes data and metadata for live and nascent tries and retained level-zero sources.
-- A receipt table's covered L0 is outside its successor checkpoint's dependency set.
-- A retained replay suffix still protects uploads it consumes before reaching that successor.
-- It includes recorded artefacts for live and nascent members, with one object per shared group key.
-- A copy uses its verified root bytes or rechecks them on a fresh read.
-- It also rechecks table bytes read for copying against those used to derive the inventory.
-- A planned source member carries its logical length and the physical version observed for that dependency.
-- An observed version includes the namespace, physical key and provider token.
-- The body supplying that member must match the plan. Two responses without version evidence rely on the immutable-object contract.
-- Existing archive receipts retain their original primary inventory. A stage verifies the inventory format recorded by its capture.
-- An archive capture registers its source metadata hold with its selected checkpoint in the same directory update.
-- A retry at an older checkpoint inherits the hold only if an open region still protects that metadata.
-- A completed archive can be verified and returned without opening serving storage.
-- A repair-backed copy admits the prior repair archive and the new destination in the same directory update.
-- A serving-store open and a transfer that materializes repair contents also name the admitted repair archive.
-- A closed region cannot start a repair read. Pending provider mutations retain their archive obligations through settlement.
-- Local archive copies and forks retain their maintenance witness through completion of admitted provider work.
-- Other admission failures end startup with the provider error.
-- The replication driver consumes the admitted cursor for that same log and boundary.
-- In the Rust partition owner, a query captures the catalog and its installed replica cut in the same partition value.
-- Ordinary recovery with a matching journal boundary seeds the cut after the driver opens its replica tail.
-- The driver advances the cut through delivered records after pending block adoption and resolution read-back buffers have drained.
-- A placed replication driver publishes its installed replica prefix and the source position covered by that same prefix together.
-- Receipt redemption uses the paired source position to know when to search for the exact attempt within an epoch.
-- On a later writer epoch, the installed recovered replica prefix establishes when the search for a previous attempt is complete.
-- A frozen attachment establishes its receipt cut when the loaded block, replica boundary and root digest match the admitted exact checkpoint.
-- An exact attempt witness in that checkpoint establishes a positive outcome at its cut.
-- Missing outcomes from a frozen checkpoint remain retryable.
-- Snapshots captured before the advance retain their original cut.
-- Exact and restored recovery modes and checkpoints without a matching journal boundary leave the cut unavailable.
-- An unrecognized replica body stops further advances on that open partition.
-- A compactor reset prepares its primary inventory from the adopted checkpoint or rebuilds it from a retained checkpoint and the held journal.
-- It publishes the replacement compacted files under fresh names before submitting the reset against the captured block and catalog.
-- The driver suspends new cuts until it adopts the replacement checkpoint.
-- Providers that defer cursor admission can still report an advisory floor and open the tail in the replication driver.
-- Exact recovery keeps its selected block. Archive recovery requires its anchor and probes only its contiguous successors.
-- A successful cursor admission does not reserve the replica suffix against later trimming.
--
-- A new leader can always reconstruct correct state from the last persisted block and the follower's in-memory state, which has been built by processing replica messages.
--
-- Claiming a replay target and awaiting it ensures the follower has fully caught up before its state is captured and it is stopped: the follower is the sole replica-log consumer and the leader consumes the source log instead, so records between the follower's position and the target would otherwise be silently skipped.
--
-- Where the follower had a pending block — a boundary read but no upload — the new leader produces that block under its OWN term, before its role is published, and adopts it once it reads an upload of that block back.
-- That upload is this term's own: an outgoing leader's upload of the same block sits below the high-water by the time it is read, and is discarded.
-- The block stays held across the cutover, and the records the follower was holding drain at the adopt.
--
-- Such a term opens the source log only once it has adopted that block, after the source position the block covers, and seeds its resolver there too.
-- The boundary can cover a position past the last applied transaction: the flush that triggered the cut, which the outgoing leader resolved without appending anything for it.

-- == The block cycle ==
--
-- A block is filled, cut, produced and adopted, and the leader term that holds the cycle runs it for this term's own blocks and for the one a promotion inherited alike.
-- Resolution is armed only while filling, so nothing is resolved between a boundary and the adopt that closes its block.
-- live_index.block_row_count and Leader.block_in_progress are the coarser view this spec's rules take of that cycle.
--
-- A cut is TWO operations — the block is PRODUCED on the boundary and ADOPTED on the confirmation coming back — which is also how a follower reads the same pair of messages.
--
-- PRODUCE, on reading the boundary back:
--   1. Finish the live index's block, yielding L0 tries — one per table that took rows in this block, and none at all for a table that took none.
--      Each L0 page retains its computed metadata summary for the metadata file and releases temporary allocations for scanning that page.
--      Each L0 page uses the table's captured column schema and the order of the leaf's rows.
--      The live trie writer gathers compatible document vectors from the leaf's selected positions when a field's layout changes during the block.
--      A block cut with no transactions in it therefore writes no tries whatsoever.
--   2. Compute the trie layout those L0 tries imply, which is what the per-table block files record and what the trie catalog will hold once the adopt has added them.
--   3. Build the per-table block files and upload one for EVERY table the table catalog knows, not only the tables from step 1.
--      Per-table cardinality sketches hash the captured live Arrow cells in physical field order and exclude logical nulls.
--      Block GC's per-table sweep depends on this — see block-gc.allium, EveryTableBlockAtCurrentIndex.
--   4. Build and persist the block file, carrying the boundary's term and its position within that term, which are what seed a restarted node's fence and term position, and stamp the database's last-upload time.
--   5. Append the upload to the replica log and await it.
--      This is the message the whole cluster closes the block on, this node included.
--      It is appended in its turn behind whatever the term has already queued, through the one ordered path every record the term writes takes, and a term ending while it is queued fails the wait.
--   The block, its L0 tries and its boundary are held from here until the adopt.
--   What is NOT installed here is the table catalog's metadata fold and the trie catalog's state, and the live index is not rolled — so a term dying between the two operations leaves the block RE-PRODUCIBLE, a second attempt at an index the catalog already holds being refused.
--   Table entries the produce DOES allocate, minting one for a table new in this block: the per-table file paths of step 3 and the block file of step 4 both record them, so they cannot wait for the adopt.
--   That allocation is what keeps re-producibility true rather than an exception to it: it is idempotent and carries no summed field, so a block produced twice mints nothing the second time and no value drifts.
--
-- ADOPT, on reading a confirmation of the held block back: see Adopting a block.
--   The role that produced the block holds it until here, because only the produce can build it; the tries and the log timestamp come off the confirming record, as a follower's do.
--   The records held behind the boundary are applied once the adopt has returned.
--   Nothing may apply a transaction between the produce's step 1 and the block adoption's write: the live tables have been snapshotted into L0 and are about to be cleared, so a transaction applied in between is written nowhere.
--   The pending-block hold is what holds a record arriving in that window, and the resolution pause is what keeps the set of held records small.
--
-- The MATCH that closes a block is on block index, storage version and storage epoch, and never on term — the term having been settled by the fence before the record reaches the match.
--
-- The producing term is not always the boundary's: a promotion produces the previous leader's pending block.
-- An upload carries the term of whoever appends it, because a leader confirms a write only on reading it back at its own term.
-- Two terms can therefore produce one block index, where the term that cut the boundary also produced it before ending: one boundary over one prefix, covering one source position.
--
-- At most ONE of those two uploads is admitted on any reader, and the log's order is what decides which.
-- Where the outgoing leader's upload PRECEDES the incoming claim, every reader closes the block on it before reading the claim — the promoting node included, which therefore inherits no block and produces nothing.
-- Where it FOLLOWS the claim, every reader has folded the newer term by the time it arrives and discards it, and the block closes on the incoming term's own upload.
-- So a block a dead leader left open always closes, on one upload or the other, and never on both.

-- == Adopting a block ==
--
-- Adopting a block is ONE operation, run by whichever role holds the block.
-- It adds the block's L0 tries to the trie catalog as of the record's log timestamp, refreshes the table catalog onto the block, folding in the live index's block metadata, and rolls the live index onto the block behind it.
-- All three are ONE write of the partition's value, under the partition's write lock, so a reader sees all of them or none.
-- The metadata is read from the finished block's live tables ahead of that write, and those tables are closed after it.
-- The leader and the follower run that one operation, not two matching sequences: what each supplies differently is the block itself — the leader passes the one its produce built and has held since, the follower one it reads from storage off the block index the boundary carried.
-- The tries and the log timestamp come off the record either way.
--
-- The block MUST be durable before it is adopted, because the adopt is what moves this node's catalog past it: a term that dies before adopting leaves the block re-producible, while a catalog past a block nobody wrote is past it for good.
-- It MUST run exactly once per block, the table catalog's refresh summing row counts.

-- == Staleness ==
--
-- A replica message whose effect has already been applied is skipped.
-- Each kind is compared against the watermark that actually records its effect, rather than against a single source-log position:
--   resolved transaction: stale if its tx_id is at or below watchers.latest_tx_id
--   tries added:          stale if its source position is at or below watchers.latest_source_msg_id
--   artefacts added:      stale if its source position is at or below watchers.latest_source_msg_id
--   artefacts removed:    stale if its source position is at or below watchers.latest_source_msg_id
--   block boundary:       stale if its block index is at or below table_catalog.current_block_index
--   block uploaded:       stale if its block index is at or below table_catalog.current_block_index
--   no-op:                stale if it carries a source position at or behind watchers.latest_source_msg_id;
--                         a bare term claim is never stale
--   tries deleted:        never stale — the removal is idempotent, so replay is a no-op
-- A stale message is skipped entirely before dispatch, which is what replaces per-kind idempotency checks within each handler.
--
-- The term fence is a SEPARATE check, run first and on the read (see Fencing): a record the fence discards reaches neither the pending-block hold nor the staleness comparison.
-- The hold runs next, so the upload that closes a held block is MATCHED there rather than compared: the adopt is what advances this node's catalog past the block.
-- An upload reaching the comparison with no block in flight is one this node has already adopted, and is stale by block index; an index the catalog has NOT reached is a fault.

rule TransitionToLeader {
    -- db.allium's view of a promotion: what it means for THIS database's pipeline.
    -- log-processor-lifecycle.allium owns the ordered steps and the recovery path; they are not duplicated here.
    when: ClaimConferredLeadership(database, term)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower

    ensures:
        -- Nothing to catch up to and no term to refuse: the claim was read like any other record, and by the time it conferred this node had already read everything before it.
        -- This runs on the read itself, which is the log's only consumer here, so nothing arrives between the claim conferring and this term leading.

        -- Stop following. No further processing after this point.
        FollowerStopped(log_processor)

        -- Capture the follower's pending block, where it saw a boundary but no upload before the claim.
        -- The read follows the stop: the follower goes on applying records until then, and one of them may be the upload that closes this very block.
        let pending_block = log_processor.pending_block

        -- Take the leader role.
        -- It resumes the SOURCE log from the durable watermark — for a term that inherits a block, the one its adopt leaves — and the replica read continues on this node from where the follower left off.
        -- Its resolver is seeded at the durable head, and it cuts on the rows already in the open block, so it cuts this block where the previous leader would have.
        log_processor.mode = Leader.created(
            -- The database's own source, borrowed for the length of this term, so this leader is externally fed iff the database is.
            external_source: database.external_source,
            leader_term: term,
            -- The claim that conferred this term is position 0 in it.
            last_term_seq: 0,
            last_resolved_src_msg_id: watchers.latest_source_msg_id,
            last_resolved_ext_token: watchers.external_source_token,
            -- True where this term inherits a block to produce: resolution stays paused until it has adopted it.
            block_in_progress: pending_block != null,
            source_delivery_resumed: pending_block = null
        )
        TxResolver.created(
            latest_completed_tx: live_index.latest_completed_tx
        )

        -- The new leader produces the block the outgoing one left behind, before it reads a source message.
        --   The block's data is already in the live index, applied before the boundary, but the block file was never persisted.
        --   The upload is appended under the INCOMING term, because a leader confirms a write only on reading it back at its own term.
        --   The block stays held. This term adopts it — and applies the records the follower was holding — on reading that upload back (LeaderAdoptsOwnBlock), which is after the role above is published and its replica read-back running.
        --   A term that ends in between hands the block on, and the role that takes it closes it on the same message this term was waiting for.
        if pending_block != null:
            ProduceBlock(log_processor, pending_block)

        -- Where this term inherits a block, delivery resumes at its adopt (LeaderAdoptsOwnBlock).
        if pending_block = null:
            SourceDeliveryResumed(after: watchers.latest_source_msg_id)

    @guidance
        -- ReplayEquivalence: the live index state after a promotion is identical regardless of how much work the follower had already done.
}

rule TransitionToFollower {
    -- A term ends itself — superseded on read-back, or failed — and this node re-opens a follower when it finds the term over.
    when: LeaderTermEnded(database)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- Everything awaiting the term is failed, and every staged-but-not-yet-applied transaction is discarded — they were never visible to an external query, and the next leader re-reads them from the source log and re-resolves them.
        -- The durable live index is untouched (live-index.allium, ResolverEnds).
        if tx_resolver != null:
            ResolverExited(tx_resolver)

        -- A block this term produced and has not adopted is carried to the new follower, which closes it on a confirmation of that block that the fence admits.
        -- It is read after the term has ended, as a promotion reads the follower's: the term goes on applying records until then, and one of them may be the upload that closes this very block.
        -- Ending the term mid-boundary leaves no block here at all: that record's apply is incomplete, so it is offered again and the follower opens the block itself, as on any other boundary.
        -- The follower continues from this node's replica position rather than being seeded with one: the read spans the role change.
        log_processor.mode = Follower.created()
}

---- Rules: Transaction Submission

rule ClientSubmitsTransaction {
    -- A client submits a transaction: it is appended to the SOURCE log, which every node shares, so this succeeds whether or not this node happens to be the leader.
    -- Static expansion requires a declared column list for a VALUES insert.
    -- A VALUES insert whose source width cannot match its declared projection proceeds to transaction resolution as SQL.
    -- A static SQL mutation with an invalid temporal literal proceeds to transaction resolution as SQL.
    -- The resolved abort records the literal planning diagnostic and consumes a transaction position.
    -- Static expansion reads the referenced argument positions and can expand an operation that supplies additional arguments.
    -- An operation missing a referenced argument proceeds to transaction resolution as SQL, where the argument failure aborts the transaction.
    -- A document write aimed at a system schema is rejected at submission.
    -- Before append, the source writer validates the entity ID of each concrete document, including documents produced by static SQL expansion.
    -- A missing ID reports the original document in the rejection data.
    when: ClientSubmitsTx(client, database, tx_ops, opts)

    requires: database.external_source = null

    ensures:
        let msg = TxMessage.created(
            system_time: opts.system_time,
            default_tz: opts.default_tz,
            user: opts.user,
            user_metadata: opts.user_metadata
        )
        msg appended to database.source_log
}

rule ClientSubmitsTransactionToExternalSourceDb {
    -- Rejected: submit is not a valid entry point for an external-source database, which assigns its own tx_id sequence off the resolved head and accepts transactions only through its source.
    -- Decided from the DATABASE's configuration, not this node's role.
    when: ClientSubmitsTx(client, database, tx_ops, opts)

    requires: database.external_source != null

    ensures:
        SubmitTxRejected(
            error_code: `xtdb/submit-tx-to-external-source-db`,
            db_name: database.name
        )
}

---- Rules: Leader Mode — resolve side

-- The leader resolves from four sources: the source log, the external source, the trie GC's publish step, and the resume of a paused block cut.
-- All but the apply of a read-back record are SUSPENDED while block_in_progress — the block-cut pause.
--
-- Resolving a transaction stages it on the resolver's queue, advancing the RESOLVED head so that later transactions read-your-writes against it, and appends it.
-- Nothing here touches the durable live index and nothing here advances the watchers: both follow from apply, on read-back.
-- Resolution therefore runs AHEAD of durability.
--
-- At the start of each source batch the leader checks the block flush timeout.
-- A batch that a block cut pauses part-way is set aside and resumed after the upload, and is reported complete only once fully drained, so that source delivery stays in step with resolution.

rule LeaderProcessesTx {
    -- Covers a current and a legacy transaction message alike: they differ only in how the payload decodes, which is below this spec's abstraction level.
    when: SourceTxMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- Resolve and stage on the resolver's queue, advancing the RESOLVED head.
        -- An aborted transaction is staged too, and still consumes a tx_id (live-index.allium, AbortTransaction).
        -- A transaction with rtdb_ordered_system_time enabled in user metadata aborts before evaluating mutations if its requested system time precedes the source message timestamp.
        -- The refused transaction uses the normally assigned system time, so it still advances the resolved head.
        -- A version-one bounded application retry carries an exclusive cutoff and a nonce in a dedicated source field.
        -- Its engine advances the stored retry clock by the maximum of the preceding retry clock and the current bounded request's log timestamp.
        -- An elapsed identity cannot become admissible after a clock regression or after removal of its aged-out receipt.
        -- The first admitted terminal outcome installs its receipt in the same replica frame as its transaction result, including an abort.
        -- A same-body retry links to that exact outcome; a different body fails with a conflict.
        -- A reader using the first receipt returns the original committed or aborted result.
        -- An invalid valid-time interval reports its two bounds as instants in the transaction failure and the durable error.
    -- An UPDATE produces a new version for a row when an assigned value changes under type-sensitive equality.
        -- SQL mutations stage compatible nested Arrow payloads with the valid-time bounds evaluated for each row.
        -- PATCH gap filling preserves the ordered valid-time pages and selects compatible document vectors.
        -- The patch projection keeps existing cells when a patch cell is null and keeps the patch's field set for an uncovered interval.
        -- A SQL mutation that targets a reserved table aborts with the table reference in the error data.
        -- An invalid SQL table projection contributes its error before dependent column binding and INSERT schema errors in the same abort.
    -- For concrete document writes, forbidden columns abort with the table reference and offending column names in source schema order.
    -- SQL insert plans report each forbidden projection as a planning error.
        -- The resolver looks up a private replay key against its ordered prefix before applying user operations.
        -- A matching marker has a validity interval overlapping the submission sample or a later instant.
        -- A successful first execution records its marker atomically with its user effects.
        -- The marker expires at the later of the permit cutoff and one hour after the admission sample.
        -- Private replay markers can be created by the resolver only and are protected from application writes and erases.
        -- A duplicate records the original transaction key in its durable outcome and skips the user operations.
        -- A caller error while looking up a private marker records a durable abort for that attempt and skips its user operations.
        -- A placed request carries a nonce and an attempt scope bound to its history and writer epoch.
        -- Resolution combines the scope with the authenticated principal and source position from the record.
        -- The resolved frame carries that identity and its transaction outcome witness in the same delta.
        let resolved = ResolveTx(tx_resolver, msg)
        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: msg.msg_id)
}

rule LeaderProcessesAttachDb {
    -- The leader asks whether the attach would be refused, and brings its own set into line when it reads the record back, as every other node does.
    -- A term superseded between resolving an attach and reading it back has therefore attached nothing.
    --
    -- The verdict accounts for what this term has already resolved as well as for the catalog, which shows the state before every database operation still queued on the resolver.
    -- The last one queued for a name says what will be there when this attach applies; with none, the catalog's own state stands.
    -- This is the layering a resolving transaction reads its own queued predecessors through, turned on the catalog rather than on tables.
    --
    -- A caller fault is resolved as a FAILED operation rather than propagating: the transaction aborts with that error, and the log carries the abort.
    when: SourceAttachDbMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        let error = CheckCanAttach(msg.db_name, msg.config)
        let resolved =
            if error = null:
                ResolveDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, Attach{db_name: msg.db_name, config: msg.config})
            else:
                ResolveFailedDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, error)
        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: msg.msg_id)
}

rule LeaderProcessesDetachDb {
    -- Mirror of LeaderProcessesAttachDb.
    when: SourceDetachDbMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        let error = CheckCanDetach(msg.db_name)
        let resolved =
            if error = null:
                ResolveDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, Detach{db_name: msg.db_name})
            else:
                ResolveFailedDbOp(tx_resolver, msg, error)
        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: msg.msg_id)
}

rule AppendTx {
    -- Hand a freshly-resolved transaction on to be appended, advance the resolve-side watermark, and cut a block if this transaction filled one.
    --
    -- src_msg_id is the source-log position this transaction sits at: a source-log transaction advances it; an external-source one passes the current position back, having none of its own.
    -- Either way it is what gets STAMPED on the transaction's replica record — see the header's note on the two watermarks.
    when: AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id)

    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = src_msg_id
        AppendReplicaMessage(resolved)

        -- Read off the applied rows, so a transaction resolved by a term that ends before it applies leaves nothing behind in the gauge.
        -- It therefore trails what is staged, and a block runs over by whatever is in flight when the count crosses.
        if live_index.block_row_count >= config.rows_per_block:
            LeaderCutsBlock(log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id, log_processor.last_resolved_ext_token)
}

rule LeaderCutsBlock {
    -- Inject the boundary in RESOLUTION order, so that it lands after this block's transactions and before the next block's, and PAUSE resolution until it is read back and its block uploaded.
    -- The pause is what keeps the set of records held behind an open block empty, and what keeps a transaction from resolving across the cut — one resolving there holds a transient over a relation the adopt removes.
    -- Block double-buffering (#4495) has to keep the pause, or replace what it buys.
    --
    -- The same event as live-index.allium's CutBlock, from the db side: this is where the boundary MESSAGE is built, because its fields come from here — the next block index off the table catalog, the resolve-side source watermark, the resume token — and live-index holds neither the watermark nor the external source.
    --
    -- One rule, not one per leader kind: the token is whatever the resolve side last saw, null throughout for a client-driven leader, so there is nothing to branch on.
    -- Reading the token from the WATCHERS would be wrong here — they advance on apply, which lags this cut.
    when: LeaderCutsBlock(latest_processed_msg_id, external_source_token)

    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    let block_index = (table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1) + 1

    ensures:
        AppendReplicaMessage(BlockBoundaryMessage.created(
            block_index: block_index,
            latest_processed_msg_id: latest_processed_msg_id,
            external_source_token: external_source_token
        ))
        log_processor.block_in_progress = true
}

rule LeaderProcessesFlushBlock {
    -- A source-log coordination message, resolved like any other.
    when: SourceFlushBlockMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- CAS check: cut the block only where the expected index matches.
        if msg.expected_block_idx != null
           and msg.expected_block_idx = (table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1):
            LeaderCutsBlock(msg.msg_id, log_processor.last_resolved_ext_token)
        else:
            -- A stale flush, or one with no expected index: nothing to cut, but the source watermark still has to reach the followers and no other record would carry it.
            AppendReplicaMessage(NoOpMessage.created(src_msg_id: msg.msg_id))

        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = msg.msg_id
}

rule LeaderProcessesTriesAdded {
    -- L0 from a block upload, L1+ from the compactor, forwarded to the replica log — where every node applies it to its own trie catalog, this one included (LeaderAppliesOwnTriesAdded).
    when: SourceTriesAddedMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        -- Forwarded whatever its storage version: every node guards the add itself, and the message is also what carries the source watermark forward.
        AppendReplicaMessage(TriesAddedMessage.created(
            storage_version: msg.storage_version,
            storage_epoch: msg.storage_epoch,
            tries: msg.tries,
            source_msg_id: msg.msg_id
        ))

        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = msg.msg_id
}

rule LeaderProcessesArtefactsAdded {
    -- Artefacts from an artefact lane, forwarded to the replica log, where every node records them on its trie catalog, this one included.
    -- An entry is accepted when its trie is live in the leader's durable catalog and its storage version and epoch are the leader's.
    -- The leader deletes the file of an entry whose trie is not live: a reader treats a missing artefact as absent, so deleting one is never wrong, and a file nothing records would otherwise wait for its trie's collection.
    -- A group-scoped entry's file is deleted only while the catalog holds none of the group's other members: one it holds may record the file, and is collected with it otherwise.
    -- An entry under another storage version or epoch, or one that does not parse, is dropped without deleting anything, the key this node would compute for it being under its own storage root.
    -- The deletes run beside resolution, never ahead of it.
    -- The message is forwarded even with no entry accepted, because it carries the source watermark forward.
    -- See: replication/src/driver.rs (accept_artefacts)
    when: SourceArtefactsAddedMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    let accepted = accepted_artefacts(msg)

    ensures:
        AppendReplicaMessage(ArtefactsAddedMessage.created(
            storage_version: msg.storage_version,
            storage_epoch: msg.storage_epoch,
            table: msg.table,
            entries: accepted,
            source_msg_id: msg.msg_id
        ))
        for entry in msg.entries
            where not accepted.contains(entry) and storage_matches(msg)
              and (entry.record.scope = trie or trie_cat/group_members(msg.table, part_group(entry.trie_key)).all(m => m.trie_key = entry.trie_key)):
            ObjectStoreDelete(trie_cat/recorded_artefact_key(msg.table, entry.trie_key, entry.record))

        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = msg.msg_id
}

rule LeaderProcessesArtefactsRemoved {
    -- Removals from an artefact lane, forwarded to the replica log, where every node removes the records from its trie catalog, this one included.
    -- Removing a record that is absent, or whose trie is gone, changes nothing, so the leader checks only the storage version and epoch, and forwards no entry under another.
    -- See: replication/src/driver.rs
    when: SourceArtefactsRemovedMessageReceived(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        AppendReplicaMessage(ArtefactsRemovedMessage.created(
            storage_version: msg.storage_version,
            storage_epoch: msg.storage_epoch,
            table: msg.table,
            entries: if storage_matches(msg): msg.entries else: [],
            source_msg_id: msg.msg_id
        ))

        log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id = msg.msg_id
}

-- The trie GC's publish step is a THIRD resolve-side source, alongside the source log and the external source: the GC hands the removal to the leader and awaits it rather than appending itself.
-- gc.allium owns that rule, including the ordering the append and the removal it publishes have to keep.
-- Two facts belong here, being properties of this pipeline rather than of the GC:
--   the GC's source is SUSPENDED while block_in_progress, so a tries-deleted can never be appended between a boundary and its upload;
--   its message is appended like any other, and the removal it carries is applied on the leader's own read-back (LeaderAppliesOwnTriesDeleted), which is where the chunk awaiting it is released.

---- Rules: Leader Mode — append

rule AppendReplicaMessage {
    -- Append the message to the replica log, in resolution order, STAMPED with this leader's term and the next position within it.
    -- Every record the term writes goes through this one ordered path, the block upload included, so the term's positions follow the log's order.
    -- The native writer requires a journal provider that supports guarded append before it stages a strict placed transaction.
    -- The quorum writer samples after encoding, fingerprinting, encryption and its own payload copy with the append lock held.
    -- The rounded upper sample must be at least the retained staging sample and strictly before the guarded cutoff.
    -- A staged strict write holds later resolution, appender commands and a full block's cut until its admitted read-back.
    -- On failure the appender submits no frame and the driver discards the term's speculative suffix before reclaiming and resolving the durable source again.
    -- Transport and quorum durability still follow dispatch and depend on the installed receiver's service bound.
    --
    -- Nothing here is atomic across messages and nothing needs to be: a superseded leader is fenced by the term its records carry, checked when it reads them back, not by an append that fails.
    --
    -- The append fixes the record's position in the log's order when it returns; its durability is reported separately, and this rule does not wait for it before the term's next append.
    -- The block upload is the exception, its caller needing the append's result.
    -- A record lost in flight is not reported here — the term's later records still land behind it.
    -- Every reader, this leader's own read-back included, meets the missing record as a gap in the term's positions and voids the rest of the term, the fold log-processor-lifecycle.allium already carries (VOIDED, ResignOnVoidedTerm, ClaimOnVoidedTerm).
    -- A lost record with nothing behind it is voided once something is appended after it, which the leader's next idle assertion supplies if no other traffic already did, taking the position right after the loss.
    when: AppendReplicaMessage(msg)

    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        msg.term_id = log_processor.leader_term
        msg.term_seq = log_processor.last_term_seq + 1
        log_processor.last_term_seq = log_processor.last_term_seq + 1
        msg appended to database.replica_log
}

---- Rules: Leader Mode — read back and apply

-- The leader is fed by this node's replica read, which continues from where the follower it replaced had got to — the same read that settles leadership, so there is no second consumer to keep in step.
-- This is where a committed transaction becomes queryable and where an external caller's durability handle completes: confirmed durably replicated AND confirmed unfenced.

rule LeaderAppliesRecord {
    -- Apply one record read back off our own replica log, fenced by TERM.
    -- The fence discards anything below this term, ends this term on anything above it, and discards a rival's losing claim at this same term, its position being behind this term's.
    -- So a record reaching here carries this term, unless it was held behind the block this term inherited and drained at the adopt, having met the fence at its own position under a lower term.
    -- The native driver matches a read-back of its own resolved transaction to the pending append at its sequence before apply or buffering.
    -- Both engines admit the outer term and sequence before fetching an oversized payload.
    -- A record at the sequence of a pending transaction must carry that transaction, including when an oversized envelope supplies its payload separately.
    -- A mismatch faults the processor, delivers the fault to a pending caller identified by the admitted term and sequence, and leaves the transaction's rows, catalog and applied progress untouched.
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(record)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    ensures:
        if log_processor.pending_block != null:
            -- The upload this term is waiting for is matched here, on block index, storage version and storage epoch and never on term, which the fence has settled already.
            if record.kind = BlockUploadedMessage
               and record.block_index = log_processor.pending_block.boundary_message.block_index
               and record.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
               and record.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
                AdoptOwnBlock(record)
            else:
                if not (record.kind = BlockUploadedMessage
                        and record.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
                        and record.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch
                        and record.block_index <= (table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1)):
                    log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records =
                        log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records.append(record)
        else:
            ApplyOwnRecord(record)
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnTx {
    -- The leader reads its own staged transaction back at its own term and IMPORTS it, from the resolver's QUEUE HEAD.
    -- Apply must find the corresponding staged transaction at the head of the resolver queue, or it faults with xtdb.indexer/leader-read-back-mismatch.
    -- The matched staged transaction supplies the catalog operation on both engines.
    -- The native driver applies the partition before the catalog operation.
    -- The Kotlin leader applies the catalog operation before importing the staged rows.
    --
    -- This is the point at which the transaction becomes queryable: the DURABLE head advances here, the watchers advance here, and an external caller's durability handle completes here — after, not before, the transaction is durable.
    --
    -- The database operation it carries is applied here, as a follower applies it on its own read — before the durability handle completes, so a caller that submitted an attach can use the database the moment its transaction returns.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = ResolvedTxMessage
    requires: record.term_id = log_processor.leader_term

    -- In the native driver, the pending append retains the caller's durability handle when apply takes the staged head.
    -- A failure applying the transaction or publishing its progress is delivered to that caller.
    let head = TakeResolvedHead(tx_resolver)
    requires: OwnReadbackMatchesHead(record, head)

    ensures:
        -- The staged head supplies the rows, catalog operation, result and source progress.
        ApplyResolvedTx(tx_resolver, head)
        if head.tx_result.committed and head.db_op != null:
            ApplyDbOpToCatalog(database.catalog, head.db_op)
        watchers.notifyTx(head.tx_result, head.src_msg_id, head.external_source_token)
        if head.external_source_token != null:
            ExternalReplicaPrefixApplied(database, head.external_source_token)
}

rule LeaderAppliesInheritedTx {
    -- A resolved transaction below this term: a previous leader's, held behind the block this term inherited and drained at the adopt.
    -- This term never resolved it, so it applies from the RECORD, as a follower applies it, and takes nothing off the resolver.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = ResolvedTxMessage
    requires: record.term_id < log_processor.leader_term

    ensures:
        if record.committed and record.db_op != null:
            ApplyDbOpToCatalog(database.catalog, record.db_op)
        ApplyResolvedTxFromRecord(record)
        if record.external_source_token != null:
            ExternalReplicaPrefixApplied(database, record.external_source_token)
}

rule LeaderUploadsBlockOnBoundary {
    -- The leader reads its OWN boundary back and PRODUCES the block (see The block cycle).
    -- By log order the durable live index now holds exactly this block's transactions and no more — which is precisely what the block-cut pause buys: nothing was resolved and applied between the boundary and here, so a durable L0 block never contains rows from a later block.
    --
    -- The block is held from here, and the adopt waits for an upload of it to come back (LeaderAdoptsOwnBlock).
    -- Native format 15 writers upload admitted L0 search files before checkpointing the block that receives them.
    -- The per-table checkpoint holds the search receipts with their primary tries, so adoption and restart restore the same coverage.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = BlockBoundaryMessage

    ensures:
        log_processor.pending_block = PendingBlock(
            boundary_msg_id: record.msg_id,
            boundary_message: record
        )
        ProduceBlock(log_processor, log_processor.pending_block)
}

rule LeaderAdoptsOwnBlock {
    -- The leader reads an upload confirming the block it holds and ADOPTS that block (see Adopting a block), then applies the records held behind the boundary.
    -- It runs the adopt a follower runs, supplying the block its own produce built and has held since — until here nothing on this node has moved past the block.
    -- The upload adopted is this term's own: an upload of that block index from a term the log has moved past is discarded before it reaches here (see Fencing).
    -- The held records go back through the leader's own apply, BELOW the fence they met on the way in, so a boundary among them produces the next block there and the records behind that one are held again.
    when: AdoptOwnBlock(record)

    ensures:
        AdoptBlock(record)

        -- The block is durable and this node is on it.
        -- Announcing that as its own event is what lets an external source confirm its upstream position without reaching into the block cycle.
        BlockBecameDurable(database, record.external_source_token)

        watchers.notifyMsg(record.latest_processed_msg_id)

        -- A block boundary is the only moment new garbage can appear.
        BlockGcSignalled()
        TrieGcSignalled()

        -- Resolution resumes, picking up any source batch set aside.
        log_processor.block_in_progress = false
        ResolutionResumed()

        let buffered = log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records
        log_processor.pending_block = null
        for held in buffered:
            HandleReplicaMessage(held)

        -- A term that inherited this block has read no source message yet, and starts here, after the watermark the adopt and the drain leave.
        -- Its resolver resumes at that same position, so the first message it resolves is the one after everything the block and the held records cover.
        if not log_processor.source_delivery_resumed:
            log_processor.source_delivery_resumed = true
            SourceDeliveryResumed(after: watchers.latest_source_msg_id)
}

rule LeaderFaultsOnUnmatchedBlockUpload {
    -- An upload reaching a leader's apply with no block in flight is a FAULT, and nothing explains it: this node holds no boundary for that block, and the fence admitted the record at or above this term.
    -- Unreachable on three counts: this term's own upload is matched against the block it holds, one from a term the log has moved past is discarded, and one from a higher term demotes this term before the record reaches a role at all.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = BlockUploadedMessage

    ensures:
        ProcessingError(msg_id: record.msg_id, error: "BlockUploaded reached apply with no block in flight")
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnTriesAdded {
    -- The leader reads its own forwarded tries-added back and registers the tries in its trie catalog, under the same storage guard every node applies and off the REPLICA record's log timestamp.
    -- By log order the add lands ahead of any boundary appended behind it, so the per-table block files a block upload writes snapshot a catalog carrying every add the log holds ahead of that boundary.
    -- The watermark advance follows the add: the compactor awaits that watermark and then recalculates its jobs off the catalog, so advancing it first would have the compactor re-select the job it has just published.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = TriesAddedMessage

    ensures:
        if record.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
           and record.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
            trie_cat/trie_catalog.addTries(record.tries, record.log_timestamp)

        watchers.notifyMsg(record.source_msg_id)
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnTriesDeleted {
    -- The leader reads its own tries-deleted back and drops the keys from its trie catalog, taking them from the RECORD, so it applies exactly what every other node applies.
    -- The chunk awaiting the append is released here, so gc.allium's publish returns with the catalog already past the keys whose files it has deleted.
    -- The record confirms the OLDEST chunk still outstanding: every tries-deleted a leader reads back is its own term's append — a higher term supersedes it and a lower one is fenced — and the log hands its own appends back in order.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = TriesDeletedMessage

    ensures:
        trie_cat/trie_catalog.deleteTries(record.table_name, record.trie_keys)
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnArtefactsAdded {
    -- The leader reads its own forwarded artefacts-added back and records the artefacts on its trie catalog, under the same storage guard every node applies.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = ArtefactsAddedMessage

    ensures:
        if record.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
           and record.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
            trie_cat/trie_catalog.withArtefacts(record.table, record.entries)

        watchers.notifyMsg(record.source_msg_id)
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnArtefactsRemoved {
    -- The leader reads its own forwarded artefacts-removed back and removes the records from its trie catalog, under the same storage guard every node applies.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = ArtefactsRemovedMessage

    ensures:
        if record.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
           and record.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
            trie_cat/trie_catalog.withoutArtefacts(record.table, record.entries)

        watchers.notifyMsg(record.source_msg_id)
}

rule LeaderAppliesOwnNoOp {
    -- Nothing to apply but the durable watermark, which is exactly what makes it lag resolution — and only where the no-op carries one, a term claim having none.
    when: ApplyOwnRecord(record)
    requires: record.kind = NoOpMessage

    ensures:
        if record.src_msg_id != null:
            watchers.notifyMsg(record.src_msg_id)
}

---- Rules: Leader Flush Timeout

rule LeaderFlushTimeout {
    -- Checked at the start of each source batch: where the current block's latest tx_id has not advanced since the previous check after flush_timeout, and this transaction has not already been flushed, the leader writes a flush to the SOURCE log.
    -- To the source log, so that the cut goes through resolution in log order like any other boundary trigger, rather than being applied out of band.
    when: BlockFlushTimeoutElapsed(database)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader

    requires: live_index.has_data

    ensures:
        FlushBlockMessage.created(
            expected_block_idx: table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1
        ) appended to database.source_log
}

---- Rules: External Source

-- An external-source leader does NOT take transactions from the source log.
-- The source is handed an indexing callback on assignment and feeds transactions in directly.
-- Flush and tries coordination still use the source log, so the leader also reads it — but transaction, attach and detach messages MUST NOT appear there, being rejected at submit time (ClientSubmitsTransactionToExternalSourceDb).

rule ExternalSourceAssigned {
    -- On assignment the external source receives an indexing callback and the resume token to start after.
    -- The token comes from the watchers, which reflect the replay of the replica log and so may be ahead of the last persisted block.
    -- A node that leads, is demoted, then leads again is assigned again on the same source, so state belonging to one assignment lives inside the assignment rather than on the source.
    when: PartitionsAssigned(database)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null

    ensures:
        ExternalSourceStarted(
            source: log_processor.external_source,
            after_token: watchers.external_source_token
        )
}

rule ExternalSourceIndexesTx {
    -- The per-transaction entry point. The source supplies an opaque resume token, an optional system time, and a writer that populates a transaction with the transformed row data.
    -- It may await the result or not; the indexing below is identical either way, and the difference is caller-side.
    --
    -- Unlike a client-driven transaction, tx_id here is assigned off the RESOLVED head, not off a source-log position and not off the durable head.
    -- system_time is smoothed strictly greater than the previous transaction's.
    --
    -- The caller's handle completes on READ-BACK (LeaderAppliesOwnTx), so it means durably replicated AND confirmed unfenced.
    -- Every path on which the transaction will never be applied MUST fail it instead, or the caller waits forever — see live-index.allium.
    --
    -- Backpressure is one transaction of lookahead plus the block-cut pause: while a block is in progress the external source is suspended, so a bursty source cannot grow the resolver's queue without bound.
    when: ExternalSourceIndexTx(external_source_token, system_time, writer)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null

    ensures:
        -- The stamped watermark MUST be the resolve-side one, not the watchers': a source transaction resolved and appended ahead of this one applies first and pushes the durable watermark past a stale stamp, at which point the watchers' monotonicity obligation is broken.
        let resolved = ResolveExternalSourceTx(
            tx_resolver, system_time, external_source_token, writer,
            src_msg_id: log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id
        )

        if external_source_token != null:
            log_processor.last_resolved_ext_token = external_source_token

        AppendTx(resolved, src_msg_id: log_processor.last_resolved_src_msg_id)
}

---- Rules: Replica Message Handling

-- A follower applies already-durable messages, so it imports straight into the durable live index and advances the watchers as it goes.
-- The TERM FENCE (see Fencing) runs on the read, above everything below: only admitted messages reach a role at all.
-- While a block is open, the pending-block handler first recognizes the upload that closes it and skips current-storage uploads for already-installed blocks.
-- The hold buffers remaining records until the pending block closes.
-- STALENESS (see Staleness) skips other messages whose effects are already applied when they reach the handlers.
-- So only unheld, non-stale messages reach the handlers below, and all of them have been admitted.

rule LogProcessorDispatchesRecord {
    -- This node's replica read: read one record, fence it, hand what it admits to whichever role is live, await it, advance.
    -- Reading one at a time and awaiting it is what leaves the term fence with a single caller.
    when: ReplicaMessageReceived(record)

    ensures:
        -- log-processor-lifecycle.allium's fold, which every reader runs alike: it folds max_leader_term upward and this node's position within that term, and returns the record's verdict.
        let verdict = FoldIntoTermPosition(log_processor, record)

        if verdict = fenced:
            -- Written by a leader the log has since moved past, so every reader discards it.
            RecordDiscarded(record)
        if verdict = discarded:
            -- Behind its term's position: a losing claim, or a duplicate.
            RecordDiscarded(record)
        if verdict = voided:
            -- A record of its term never reached the log, so this one and the rest of that term are consumed and never applied.
            RecordDiscarded(record)
            -- A leader has nothing further to read back at or below the voided term, so its term ends here.
            if log_processor.mode = Leader:
                LeaderTermEnded(database)
        if verdict = admitted:
            -- A record above the term a leader is leading has superseded that term (see Fencing).
            -- The term ends where the record is read, and the record is then offered to the follower that replaces it.
            if log_processor.mode = Leader and record.term_id > log_processor.leader_term:
                LeaderTermEnded(database)

            HandleReplicaMessage(record)

        -- One advance per record, whatever became of it — a role change continues from it.
        -- A record the fence discarded or a role held advances it all the same, so the read never stalls on one.
        -- A faulting apply does not advance it: it ends the read.
        log_processor.latest_replica_msg_id = record.msg_id
}

rule FollowerHoldsRecordBehindPendingBlock {
    -- A record that arrives while a block is open is STORED, and nothing else — see PendingBlock.
    -- Subscribes to the same dispatch event as HandleReplicaMessage and overrides it, as FollowerHandlesBlockUploaded does for the record that closes the block.
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower
    requires: log_processor.pending_block != null
    requires: not (msg.kind = BlockUploadedMessage
                   and msg.block_index = log_processor.pending_block.boundary_message.block_index
                   and msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
                   and msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch)
    requires: not (msg.kind = BlockUploadedMessage
                   and msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
                   and msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch
                   and msg.block_index <= (table_catalog.current_block_index ?? -1))

    ensures:
        log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records =
            log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records.append(msg)
}

rule HandleReplicaMessage {
    -- Applies an admitted message on a follower: the staleness check, and then the apply.
    -- The pending-block hold overrides this rule while a block is open (FollowerHoldsRecordBehindPendingBlock, FollowerHandlesBlockUploaded), and LeaderAppliesRecord takes the message where a leader is live.
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(msg)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower

    ensures:
        if msg.kind = ResolvedTxMessage:
            live_index.importTx(msg)
            if msg.committed:
                if msg.db_op.kind = Attach:
                    database.catalog.attach(msg.db_op.db_name, msg.db_op.config)
                if msg.db_op.kind = Detach:
                    database.catalog.detach(msg.db_op.db_name)
            -- The stamped position where present.
            -- On an older record it is absent, and the fallback differs by database: an external-source tx_id is an independent counter, so it says nothing about the source log and the tracked watermark has to stand in; a source-log tx_id IS a source-log position.
            let effective_src_msg_id =
                msg.src_msg_id
                    ?? (if database.external_source != null:
                            watchers.latest_source_msg_id
                        else: msg.tx_id)
            watchers.notifyTx(msg.result, effective_src_msg_id, msg.external_source_token)

        if msg.kind = TriesAddedMessage:
            if msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
               and msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
                trie_cat/trie_catalog.addTries(msg.tries, msg.log_timestamp)
            watchers.notifyMsg(msg.source_msg_id)

        if msg.kind = ArtefactsAddedMessage:
            if msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
               and msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
                trie_cat/trie_catalog.withArtefacts(msg.table, msg.entries)
            watchers.notifyMsg(msg.source_msg_id)

        if msg.kind = ArtefactsRemovedMessage:
            if msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
               and msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch:
                trie_cat/trie_catalog.withoutArtefacts(msg.table, msg.entries)
                -- The lane deletes the objects from the store; each node drops its own cached copies.
                for entry in msg.entries:
                    NodeCacheInvalidate(trie_cat/recorded_artefact_key(msg.table, entry.trie_key, entry.record))
            watchers.notifyMsg(msg.source_msg_id)

        if msg.kind = TriesDeletedMessage:
            -- Idempotent removal, so unconditional: no storage guard and no staleness check.
            trie_cat/trie_catalog.deleteTries(msg.table_name, msg.trie_keys)

        if msg.kind = BlockBoundaryMessage:
            -- The follower opens a pending block and uses the hold above for subsequent records other than the closing upload and current-storage uploads for already-installed blocks.
            log_processor.pending_block = PendingBlock(msg.msg_id, msg)

        if msg.kind = NoOpMessage and msg.src_msg_id != null:
            watchers.notifyMsg(msg.src_msg_id)
}

rule FollowerHandlesBlockUploaded {
    -- The follower holding a block reads the matching upload and runs the adopt a leader runs (see Adopting a block), supplying a block it reads from the object store.
    -- The block does not come from the message; the message is just a signal.
    -- Subscribes to the same dispatch event as HandleReplicaMessage, which has no upload branch of its own, and overrides it for the pending-block case.
    -- The record has already met the fence, on the read and ahead of the hold, so the match is all that is left to test (see Fencing).
    when: HandleReplicaMessage(msg)
    requires: msg.kind = BlockUploadedMessage
    requires: log_processor.mode = Follower
    requires: log_processor.pending_block != null
    requires: msg.block_index = log_processor.pending_block.boundary_message.block_index
    requires: msg.storage_version = storage_format.storage_version
    requires: msg.storage_epoch = storage_format.storage_epoch

    ensures:
        -- The adopt, over the block at that index read from the object store.
        AdoptBlock(msg)

        if config.automatic_compaction:
            CompactorSignalled()

        -- The block's covered source position, ahead of the drain below: the held records carry later positions, so behind them this one would go backwards (see the watchers' monotonicity obligations).
        watchers.notifyMsg(msg.latest_processed_msg_id)

        -- Close the block, then apply what it was holding; their own notifications are what advance the watermarks past this position.
        -- They re-enter BELOW the fence, each having met it at its own position in the log on the way in.
        let buffered = log_processor.pending_block.buffered_records
        log_processor.pending_block = null
        for record in buffered:
            HandleReplicaMessage(record)
}

---- Rules: Compaction Orchestration

-- The signal-driven wake rule covers automatic compaction.
-- In native manual mode, compaction schedules jobs only while a compact-all request is pending.
-- Job selection and the merge algorithm are compaction.allium's.
-- The compactor writes L1+ tries to the SOURCE log, and the leader forwards them to the replica log.

rule CompactorWakesUp {
    when: CompactorSignalled()

    let jobs = compaction/available_jobs(trie_cat/trie_catalog)

    ensures:
        for job in jobs:
            StartCompactionJob(job)
}

rule CompactionJobWritesOutput {
    when: StartCompactionJob(job)

    ensures:
        -- The data file is uploaded first; the meta file's presence is the completion marker.
        -- A primary rewrite retains the accepted event identity and can publish replacement history and forward witnesses with its new locations.
        -- Each output trie carries the records of the artefacts built from its data file before the tries are appended (fts.allium: SearchJobRuns), so it becomes live with them.
        -- The tries go to the source log, and the leader forwards them to the replica log.
        TriesAddedMessage.created(tries: job.output_tries) appended to database.source_log
}

rule CompactionJobConfirmed {
    -- The tries-added has round-tripped through the log and is now in the trie catalog.
    -- Job selection naturally sees the updated catalog for the next cycle, so this rule asserts the event and adds no postconditions of its own.
    when: CompactionOutputVisible(job)
}

---- Rules: Query Serving

rule ClientOpensSnapshot {
    -- External queries open at the DURABLE head, never the resolved head.
    -- A query must never see a transaction that could vanish if the leader crashed before its replica-log write landed, nor one a newer-term leader superseded.
    when: ClientOpensSnapshot(client, database)

    ensures:
        Snapshot.created(
            as_of: live_index.latest_completed_tx
        )
}

rule ExternalSourceLearnsDurableExtent {
    -- Confirmation permits the upstream to discard its history through the token.
    -- An adopted block supplies a recoverable token to the source.
    -- The source's protocol determines how that token bounds confirmation.
    when: BlockBecameDurable(database, external_source_token)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null
    requires: external_source_token != null

    ensures:
        DurableExtentAdvanced(
            source: log_processor.external_source,
            token: external_source_token
        )
}

rule ExternalSourceLearnsReplayExtent {
    -- Synced local journals retain the durable prefix through restart.
    -- Synced local storage retains the checkpoint files used to open that prefix.
    -- The leader can supply the token from applied read-back before a block adopts it.
    when: ExternalReplicaPrefixApplied(database, external_source_token)
    requires: log_processor.mode = Leader
    requires: database.external_source != null
    requires: RetainsSyncedJournalsAndCheckpoints(database)

    ensures:
        RecoverableReplayExtentAdvanced(source: log_processor.external_source, token: external_source_token)
}

---- Rules: Error Handling

rule ProcessingErrorHaltsNode {
    -- A genuine ingestion fault marks the watchers as failed and surfaces the cause according to the engine's wait rules.
    --
    -- A SUPERSESSION is not one of these, and neither is an interruption of the term: see LeaderSuperseded.
    -- Poisoning the watchers on a routine leadership handover would surface it to queries as a failed database.
    when: ProcessingError(msg_id, error)

    ensures:
        watchers.notifyError(error)
        NodeMarkedUnhealthy()
}

---- Contracts

contract TableEntries {
    -- Obligations on the entries a Block carries: each table's identity, and where its files live.
    -- These are about an entry's history across successive blocks, so they cannot be expressed as single-snapshot invariants over entity state.

    @invariant SlugFixedWhenFirstRecorded
        -- A table's slug is chosen once, by the first block that records the table, and every later block carries it forward unchanged.

    @invariant OidFixedWhenTheTableIsFirstSeen
        -- A table's oid is allocated when the catalog first learns of the table, and every later block carries it forward unchanged.
        -- - The catalog learns of a table when a transaction writes to it, which is earlier than the block that first records it.

    @invariant EveryNodeAllocatesTheSameOid
        -- Two nodes reach the same oid for a table without it passing between them.
        -- - Allocation follows the order tables first appear in the log, and the tables of one transaction are allocated in name order, so replaying the same log is enough to agree.

    @invariant EveryTableTheBlockNamesHasAnEntry
        -- A block records an entry for each table it names, taking the entry from the preceding block wherever that block held one.

    @invariant NamesOnlyBlockUsesLegacySlugs
        -- A block carrying names alone resolves its tables through the escaped form of their names.
        -- Oids for those tables are derived in name order, so every node resolving the same block resolves the same entries.

    @invariant NewlyAllocatedSlugsAreUnique
        -- A new table takes its escaped name when the slug is free.
        -- If that slug already belongs to a table, the new table takes the first unused slug formed by appending a tilde and a positive integer in increasing order.
        -- The catalog allocates slugs in table name order within each transaction.

    @invariant SlugPrecedesTheFirstWrite
        -- A table's slug is fixed before anything is written under its path.
        -- - A live table takes its entry when the live index creates it, so the L0 trie it writes at the block boundary lands under the slug the block then records for it.

    @invariant OnlyARecordedTableHasABlockFile
        -- A per-table block file exists for exactly the tables the block records.
        -- - A table allocated but not yet written has an entry and no file.
}

contract ExternalSourceConfirmation {
    -- Obligations on an ExternalSource that confirms progress upstream.
    -- These are about the ordering of confirmations against block durability over time, so they cannot be expressed as single-snapshot invariants over entity state.

    @invariant ConfirmedPositionIsRecoverable
        -- Everything the upstream has been told it may discard is recoverable without it, so recovery is the last block plus the upstream resumed from the token that block carries — one artefact, not two.
        -- - Ordinarily this bounds the confirmed position by the last durable block's token.
        --   A source MAY confirm beyond it over a range that holds nothing of ours; that is the source's rule to state and to justify, per pgsrc.allium.

    @invariant ConfirmationNeverNamesAnUnappliedTransaction
        -- No confirmed position covers a transaction that has not been applied.
        -- - Below the durable extent this holds by construction rather than by a check: a boundary is appended after the transactions it covers, so by the time a block is durable every transaction its token names has already been applied.
        --   The gate can therefore only hold confirmation back, never push it past apply.
        -- - Above the extent it holds because the only positions a source may confirm there are ones it has established name no transaction at all.

    @invariant ConfirmationIsLeaderOnly
        -- Only the leader for a database holds its ExternalSource, so only the leader confirms.
        -- A follower has no upstream connection and no position of its own, so a leadership handover cannot produce two nodes confirming different positions to the same upstream.
}

contract OfflineSnapshotExport {
    @invariant CompletedBlockRequired
        -- An export captures one completed block from a database opened with indexing and compaction disabled.
        -- A database with no completed block yields no export.

    @invariant CapturedRecoveryFiles
        -- The manifest contains the captured block catalog, its per-table block files and the data/meta pairs of its live and nascent tries.
        -- Every copied object retains its original bytes.

    @invariant EpochZeroDestination
        -- The destination is a fresh single-partition storage root at epoch zero, under an export directory named by the captured block and export time.

    @invariant RetryableCopies
        -- A failed export retains copies already completed.
        -- Repeating a copy accepts an identical destination object.
        -- Cancellation takes effect between object copies after the current copy finishes.

    @invariant DryRunManifest
        -- A dry run returns the file manifest and leaves the destination unchanged.
}

contract CompactorReset {
    @invariant ExclusiveMaintenance
        -- The operator keeps every other node, client and upstream writer stopped throughout the reset.
        -- The maintenance node catches up through a completed block with indexing enabled and compaction and automatic garbage collection disabled.

    @invariant InstalledL0Inventory
        -- A reset without a retained export uses the L0 identities bound by the installed checkpoint and its table inventories.
        -- That plan verifies the recorded data and metadata pairs before acceptance.
        -- The observed data length matches the checkpoint's recorded length where present.
        -- An incomplete inventory refuses preparation.

    @invariant RetainedPrimaryReplay
        -- A native local maintenance node can rebuild its adopted checkpoint using the detached export of its persisted repair anchor and the durable journal it holds.
        -- The persisted anchor binds the export block, digest and journal boundary.
        -- Replay verifies both endpoints and stops at the adopted transaction head and table registry.
        -- Rebuilt pairs are synchronized before the successor is published.
        -- Rebuilt compacted pairs use names disjoint from prior resets and normal compactor outputs.
        -- Original L0 identities remain available to bind the inputs of source-order replay.

    @invariant PublicationPrecedesDeletion
        -- A reset with a completed transaction publishes the replacement catalog in a new block.
        -- A coordinated store records the compacted file identities and required block and naming epoch before publication.
        -- The adopted root must cover the required block and naming epoch in the selected journal, and its catalog and checkpoint must have withdrawn the named tries, before the leader confirms their removal.
        -- Physical retirement follows the installed removal receipt and coordinated catalog floor.
        -- An exclusive reset on an uncoordinated store deletes each compacted trie's metadata before its data after adoption.

    @invariant CapturedCatalogIdentity
        -- A reset prepared from an earlier catalog or block is rejected if either changes before acceptance.
        -- A replayed replacement also requires an empty live block and the same installed and live transaction head as the replay target.

    @invariant InterruptedResetRetainsRecovery
        -- A reset interrupted before its block is adopted retains all compacted files.
        -- An interruption during retirement leaves the published replacement catalog and a durable retirement intent for each unsettled batch on a coordinated store.
        -- Collection can finish eligible reset retirements after restart and after earlier reader tickets drain.

    @invariant SuccessorJournalCatalogCut
        -- A native node that reopens a recovered checkpoint in a fresh journal initially leaves the catalog cut untracked.
        -- Discoverable recovery can publish a cut after adopting a checkpoint in the current journal whose boundary the installed replica prefix covers.

    @invariant DryRunPlan
        -- A dry run performs the initial catch-up flush and reports the prospective primary catalog and retirements.
        -- Its plan leaves the catalog and files as they stood after that catch-up.
}

contract OrderedIndexReplay {
    @invariant AdoptedEventCoverage
        -- Ordered-index maintenance checks its flushed inputs against the captured table's adopted L0 inventory.
        -- If a data object is missing, a native local database can rebuild from a retained journal from the first block through the captured checkpoint.
        -- The journal admits the captured boundary and transaction head, confirms the intervening blocks, and reproduces the event count of each recorded nonempty block.
        -- Publication requires complete inventory and total row coverage.

    @invariant ErasureBeforePublication
        -- A journal rebuild identifies the last erase of each entity across the captured inputs before projecting events into the ordered index.
        -- The rebuilt historical roots exclude events from before that erase.
}

contract LocalCheckpointRepair {
    @invariant ExactLocalAnchor
        -- The operator selects a known-good base and a later target by block number and root SHA-256.
        -- The base binds all its table blocks and has its live and nascent primary data and metadata available, including when retained as a separate snapshot export.
        -- The same stopped local replica journal covers both checkpoint boundaries.
        -- Repair holds the journal's writer lock throughout the rebuild.

    @invariant RetainedRepairBase
        -- With the serving journal locked, the operator can install an independently stored export of the current checkpoint as its retained repair base.
        -- Admission verifies the serving and exported root publications, the export's complete primary inventory, and the matching record in the local journal.
        -- Installation follows synchronization of the exported files and their directory entries.
        -- The store preserves block catalogs, per-table block files and offloaded replica payloads at and beyond that block across restarts.
        -- An operator advances protection by installing a newer verified base in the same replica journal.
        -- The collector synchronizes the replacement anchor before removing files covered by the previous anchor.
        -- A repair can reopen the persisted anchor under the original journal lock and select it as its base.
        -- The detached checkpoint must match the anchor's block, digest and replica boundary.

    @invariant ReplayThroughSelectedCut
        -- The rebuild copies the base primary inventory and replays admitted resolved transactions through the exact target boundary.
        -- The rebuilt checkpoint has the selected target's transaction head and table registry.
        -- A missing or incompatible boundary, missing source dependency or unsupported database effect prevents publication.

    @invariant DetachedPublication
        -- Repair stages a separate local store with its own primary inventory and reads back the completed checkpoint and bound table blocks.
        -- Publication exposes that store with the rebuilt checkpoint's digest after the rebuild and read-back succeed.
        -- Activating the rebuilt store uses a fresh log at a higher epoch.
}

contract StandaloneCompactor {
    @invariant FollowerCompaction
        -- A compactor-only node follows admitted replica progress and compacts the available catalog using the ordinary deterministic job selection.
        -- It remains ineligible for leadership and exposes its configured health endpoint.

    @invariant SourcePublication
        -- Completed compaction outputs are published to the database source log.
        -- A separate leader applies those notifications before the compactor reports the jobs complete.
        -- The source provider must admit the compactor alongside the writer.
}

---- Actor Declarations

actor ClientActor {
    -- Minimal identification: any Client is a valid actor for the surfaces below.
    identified_by: Client where true
}

---- Surfaces

surface MaintenanceCommands {
    facing operator: ClientActor
    context db: Database

    contracts:
        fulfils OfflineSnapshotExport
        fulfils CompactorReset
        fulfils LocalCheckpointRepair
        fulfils StandaloneCompactor
}

surface TransactionSubmission {
    facing caller: ClientActor

    context db: Database

    provides:
        ClientSubmitsTx(caller, db, tx_ops, opts)
            when db.external_source = null

    @guarantee OrderedProcessing
        -- Transactions are processed in the order they appear in the source log.

    @guarantee SubmitIsRoleAgnostic
        -- Submission appends to the shared source log, so it succeeds from any node regardless of which node leads this database.
        -- Whether the transaction has been INDEXED is a separate question, answered by awaiting its tx_id on the watchers.
}

surface QueryAccess {
    facing caller: ClientActor

    context db: Database

    provides:
        ClientOpensSnapshot(caller, db)

    @guarantee SnapshotIsolation
        -- A snapshot sees a consistent point-in-time view.
        -- It includes every transaction up to the durable head — those the leader has read BACK off its own replica log and applied.
        -- Resolved-but-not-yet-applied transactions are NOT visible, and neither are the writes of a leader a newer term superseded.

    @guarantee ExecutionReadsOneSnapshot
        -- A single query execution opens one snapshot of this database and derives from it every answer it gives about this database: the schema it resolves names against, the oids `::regclass` resolves through, and the rows it reads.
        -- An execution spanning several databases holds one such snapshot per database.

    @guarantee ColumnarScanProjection
        -- A scan preserves compatible list and struct columns as Arrow arrays when projecting selected rows.
        -- A scan can read compatible leaf counts, bounds, and Bloom bytes directly from metadata arrays.
        -- Equality selection on a keyword ID includes the native and reference persisted digests.
        -- For columnar query output, stacked filters apply in plan order to the surviving vectors and row positions.
        -- A columnar limit applies its offset and row count to batches and slices compatible Arrow arrays.
        -- DISTINCT and set operations with columnar inputs select their output positions from those Arrow batches.
        -- Their key index retains Arrow batches and compares compatible nulls, booleans, signed integers, text and binary cells at their indexed positions.
        -- Compatible global counts and floating-point sums and averages reduce Arrow columns and emit scalar result vectors.
        -- Compatible grouping columns can use the first Arrow position of each key and emit scalar aggregates in that first-seen order.
        -- Grouped output keeps the configured transport row limit.
        -- Scalar aggregates read their operands as needed and write compatible results as Arrow columns.
        -- List aggregates collect selected Arrow positions after applying filters and distinctness, and sort Arrow keys when the aggregate specifies an order.
        -- Percentile aggregates order each group's selected positions and read the ranked arguments from their source vectors.
        -- A compatible scalar membership join indexes the build key vectors and appends nullable Boolean marks to the probe batches.
        -- Scalar membership uses the raw vector hash to select candidates before comparing them.
        -- A spilled scalar membership join retains the probe payloads and the build's global null facts through Arrow partition replay.
        -- An Arrow join can evaluate a residual predicate from borrowed cells on either side while gathering the result payloads from the input batches.
        -- Join partitions retain flat and nested layouts in Arrow IPC, including selected cells whose physical fields differ.
        -- A shared mark can replay its candidate Arrow pages to evaluate arbitrary Boolean comparisons.
        -- Materialized bindings retain their semantic page boundaries and physical fields in Arrow IPC.
        -- Outer joins track unmatched build positions through partition replay.
        -- A correlated query retains its outer Arrow row position and gathers its output from the selected dependent positions.
        -- Short-circuiting correlated queries observe the dependent plan's semantic pages.
        -- UNNEST over compatible Arrow list columns expands the selected child positions and gathers their parent payloads by position.
        -- A column table over a compatible Arrow list parameter reads the bound row's child slice as one semantic page.
        -- Its columnar result is split to the transport row limit after the operators have processed the complete page.
        -- Scalar evaluation in columnar projections, filters, aggregate filters, and evaluated UNNEST can borrow the cells demanded by the expression.
        -- A nested field read can select its child from an input struct vector.
        -- Computed outputs publish Arrow columns or positions in arrays with differing physical fields.
        -- Computed Arrow projections validate decimal precision on the selected result cells, including nested decimals.
        -- Supported physical sort keys are read from their vectors in memory and during Arrow spill replay.
        -- A projection over a direct scan retains its compatible input columns when other expressions use scalar evaluation.
        -- Adjacent compatible projections can pass computed scalar results as Arrow columns, including into a mutation's document projection.
        -- A literal table with stable scalar columns builds one Arrow batch before its consumers run.
        -- Literal tables and stacked scalar projections gather output up to the configured transport row limit before encoding it.
        -- A list expression over inputs with a shared compatible layout interleaves the child values in Arrow.
        -- A struct expression over compatible Arrow fields retains the child arrays.
        -- Selecting a compatible struct child combines its validity with the parent validity in Arrow.
        -- A projected field missing from some pages uses Arrow nulls when the available columns share a layout or form a scalar dense union.
        -- A projected dense union with compatible children retains its Arrow array when it already matches the result layout.
        -- A compatible projected array can be aligned to the result's nested fields and union legs in Arrow.
        -- When selected cells need layout alignment before they can share one array, the scan retains their source vectors and positions.
        -- Reading a selected struct during evaluation preserves which keys its source has.
        -- Compatible fixed-size lists stay in Arrow through sparse selection and output.
        -- A sparse selection of compatible in-memory maps also stays in Arrow.
        -- A delegated scan sends selected source vectors in Arrow IPC in their scan order.
        -- Each fragment frame preserves its source fields and the coordinator can filter its Arrow vectors by position.
}

---- Deferred Specifications

deferred ResolveTx
  -- Resolve a source-log transaction — the put, delete and erase logic — stage it on the resolver's queue and advance the RESOLVED head.
  -- Covers the legacy payload form too.
  -- See: live-index.allium CommitTransaction / AbortTransaction
deferred ResolveExternalSourceTx
  -- Assign the next tx_id off the RESOLVED head, smooth the system time strictly greater than the previous transaction's, run the caller's writer against a fresh transaction, and stage the result with the caller's durability handle.
  -- See: live-index.allium CommitTransaction
deferred ResolveDbOp
  -- Resolve an attach or detach as a transaction carrying the database operation, for the catalog to be brought into line with when the record is read back.
  -- Smooth its source-log time strictly past the resolved head, as for an ordinary transaction without an explicit system time.
deferred ResolveFailedDbOp
  -- Resolve an attach or detach that would be refused, as an ABORTED transaction carrying the caller fault that refused it.
  -- It advances the resolved system time under the same rule as a successful attach or detach.
deferred CheckCanAttach
  -- Whether an attach would be refused, and with which caller fault.
  -- Answered from the catalog's own records folded together with the database operations the resolver still holds: the last one queued for a name says what will be there when this attach applies, and with none the catalog's own records stand.
  -- Reaches its verdict without changing anything.
deferred CheckCanDetach
  -- Whether a detach would be refused, answered the same way.
deferred ApplyDbOpToCatalog
  -- Bring the catalog into line with a committed database operation, swallowing a caller fault: the transaction has already committed, so a node that cannot carry the instruction out holds none of a database the cluster agrees exists.
  -- See: database-lifecycle.allium AdmitInstructionCreatesEntry, RemoveBeginsTeardown
deferred TakeResolvedHead
  -- Remove and return the oldest staged transaction for the caller to verify against the read-back it is about to apply.
  -- A failure after removal still reaches the caller's durability handle (see LeaderAppliesOwnTx).
  -- See: live-index.allium ApplyResolvedTx
deferred OwnReadbackMatchesHead -- see: ../../allium/live-index.allium
  -- Match the record to the oldest staged transaction in the current term.
  -- The native driver requires the pending append's encoded transaction and the staged transaction it owns.
  -- The Kotlin leader compares the transaction ID with its staged head.
deferred ApplyResolvedTx
  -- Apply on read-back: import the head's rows into the durable live tables, advance the DURABLE head to it, and complete its durability handle.
  -- See: live-index.allium ApplyResolvedTx
deferred ProduceBlock
  -- Produce the block a boundary cut: finish the live index's block, build and upload the per-table block files and the block file, and append the upload under this term.
  -- See: The block cycle, above.
deferred AdoptBlock
  -- Adopt a block a confirmation names: add its L0 tries to the trie catalog, refresh the table catalog onto the block, and roll the live index onto the block behind it, in one write of the partition's value.
  -- See: Adopting a block, above.
deferred ApplyResolvedTxFromRecord
  -- Apply a resolved transaction this term did not resolve: import the record's rows into the durable live tables, advance the DURABLE head to it, and report it to the watchers from the record.
deferred FoldIntoTermPosition
  -- Fold one record into this node's highest term read and its position within that term, and return the verdict: fenced, discarded, voided or admitted.
  -- See: log-processor-lifecycle.allium, Rules: fold a record into the term position

---- Open Questions

open question "Which already-applied query bases remain available after an ingestion failure?"
    -- ProcessingErrorHaltsNode and the pinned JVM Watchers fail every later await.
    -- The native progress monitor serves satisfied bases and reports the recorded ingestion fault to unmet waiters.
    -- The native database owner reports a selected non-interrupted peer fault before requesting worker shutdown.
    -- The object-boundaries contract requires wait conditions to remain re-evaluable.

open question "Does the idle flush deadline advance the block catalog when the open block has no rows?"
    -- LeaderFlushTimeout requires a block with data.
    -- The pinned JVM BlockFlusher advances idle empty blocks to maintain log retention anchors, including after the persisted transaction has stopped changing.

open question "Which node-local caller failures during committed database-operation apply terminate ingestion?"
    -- ApplyDbOpToCatalog swallows a caller fault.
    -- The pinned JVM follower does so after applying the transaction, while LeaderLogProcessor applies the database operation before the transaction and propagates the fault.

open question "How should operator-selected skipped transactions be represented in this processing model?"
    -- The resolver persists diagnostic operations under skipped-txs before staging an aborted xt.txs row carrying xtdb/skipped-tx.
    -- For a native transaction envelope the diagnostic copy omits user metadata, retry guards and the placed attempt seed.
    -- The resolver establishes the original placed outcome from the unmodified source record.
    -- Each ordered source transaction carries the raw append observation and the greatest such observation through its predecessor.
    -- The receipt clock advances atomically with the transaction outcome in the replica record and is restored at checkpoint recovery.
    -- New transaction metadata is stored in finite-lived rows outside durable transaction-result history.
    -- Placed intake retains submitted bytes through installation of their replica outcome.
    -- Intake release follows the applied source prefix even when the holder receives no further submissions.
    -- Ordered erasures remove eligible payloads from later reads and compaction products without changing the transaction coordinate.
    -- A placed outcome record contains exactly one put naming the current attempt.
    -- A deployment admitted to the versioned outcome format may also carry bounded erasures of distinct expired outcomes.
    -- Read-back rejects erasure of the current attempt, an extra outcome, and any multi-row outcome in the original format.
    -- Physical retirement also requires successor coverage and release or revocation of each earlier immutable copy.
    -- Its transaction ID is the source position and its system time is smoothed against the resolved head.
    -- The skip takes effect before decoding transaction operations and uses the ordinary append, read-back and apply pipeline.

open question "What processing obligations apply to a database opened with indexing disabled?"
    -- The pinned database opens its persisted state and logs, accepts source submissions, and runs no log processor or external source.
    -- This model currently requires a log processor for each database.

-- Black box helpers for artefacts:
--   accepted_artefacts(msg)  — the entries whose trie the leader's durable catalog holds live, under the leader's storage version and epoch
--   part_group(trie_key)     — the partition group a tiered trie belongs to (trie-cat.allium: PartGroup)
--   storage_matches(msg)     — whether the message's storage version and epoch are the leader's
--   ObjectStoreDelete(key)   — as in gc.allium
--   NodeCacheInvalidate(key) — drop this node's cached copies of the object, leaving the store's untouched (storage/src/cache.rs: BufferPool::invalidate)
